WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:24.980
<v Ira Glass>When I was in my 20s, I worked as a temp typist on the overnight shift. And it was one of my favorite jobs I ever had. I showed up at work at midnight. Downtown was completely dark. Nobody on the streets at all. And I'd take the elevator to the, you know, 20th floor or whatever it was. And it would be all lit up and bustling with activity, dozens of people rushing around. Usually we were hustling out some kind of legal brief or exhibit for court the next day.
<v Ira Glass>二十多岁时，我曾做过夜班兼职打字员。那是我最喜欢的工作之一。我午夜上班，市中心一片漆黑，街上空无一人。我坐电梯到20楼左右，那里灯火通明，忙碌异常，几十个人匆忙走动。我们通常是在赶制第二天的法庭简报或证据材料。

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:42.300
<v Ira Glass>I loved being up when the rest of the world was asleep. And the next morning, when I would walk to the subway as the sun was coming up, I loved knowing that everybody else on the street was dragging themselves to their jobs and I was done for the day. It was like I was part of a secret society that's alive in the middle of the night.
<v Ira Glass>我喜欢在全世界沉睡时醒着。第二天早上，当我在日出时分走向地铁，我很享受这种感觉：街上其他人正拖着疲惫的身体去上班，而我已经结束了一天的工作。我就像是一个在深夜里活跃的秘密组织成员。

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.040
<v Austin>OK. Everybody, go home. We're about to start this.
<v Austin>好了，各位，回家吧。我们要开始录制了。

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:59.550
<v Ira Glass>It's a regular thing for Austin and his high school friends at night. The pile into a Honda Odyssey minivan. They remove the backseats and drive around the Northern California suburbs, fake-kidnapping their buddies. 15 of them.
<v Ira Glass>这对奥斯汀和他的高中朋友们来说是晚上的常态。他们挤进一辆本田奥德赛面包车，拆掉后座，在北加州的郊区兜风，假装绑架他们的伙伴。一共15个人。

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.230
<v Friend 1>Open the door! Open the door!
<v Friend 1>开门！开门！

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:03.440
<v Friend 2>Did you get him?
<v Friend 2>抓住他了吗？

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.370
<v Friend 3>Get in the back.
<v Friend 3>坐到后面去。

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:09.630
<v Friend 2>Everybody in?
<v Friend 2>都上来了吗？

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:11.070
<v Friend 1>Opa!
<v Friend 1>奥帕！

00:01:11.070 --> 00:01:25.740
<v Friend 1>The fake kidnapping is all part of a tradition that they've made up. They call it "going out living" or "living a little." It's not the weekend. The whole point of this is do this on a boring Monday or Tuesday or Thursday night. A school night when other people aren't out having fun.
<v Friend 1>这种假绑架是他们自创传统的一部分。他们称之为“出去生活”或“活出点滋味”。这不是在周末，重点就在于要在无聊的周一、周二或周四晚上做这件事。在别人都不出来玩的工作日晚上。

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:41.360
<v Friend 1>There's no destination. It's basically just glorified cruising. They drive around for hours, talking about whatever, singing along to the music. [SINGING]
<v Friend 1>没有目的地，本质上就是高级版的兜风。他们开车转悠好几个小时，随心所欲地聊天，跟着音乐唱歌。[歌唱]

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:51.980
<v Friend 1>They bring their minivan to the top of a hill and put it in neutral, and then they all rock back and forth inside the van in unison until finally it rolls down the hill.
<v Friend 1>他们把车开到山顶，挂上空挡，然后大家在车里齐声前后晃动，直到车子最终滑下山坡。

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:56.240
<v Friend 3>Whoa!
<v Friend 3>哇！

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:09.180
<v Ira Glass>And then sometime after 1 AM, Ian, the driver, pulls out the soundtrack to the Disneyland ride Space Mountain. He's worked out a way to drive in sync with the soundtrack, to simulate the actual ride.
<v Ira Glass>凌晨1点多，司机伊恩会拿出迪士尼太空山游乐项目的原声带。他摸索出了一种配合音乐节奏驾驶的方法，以此来模拟真实的游乐体验。

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.190
<v Austin>Yeah. We're in the car driving very slowly up this dark hill.
<v Austin>是的，我们在车里在这座漆黑的山坡上开得很慢。

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:15.080
<v Ira Glass>This is Austin.
<v Ira Glass>这是奥斯汀。

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:23.500
<v Austin>The headlights are very low. This kind of eerie music. Everyone's a little bit nervous, because you're about to do something-- probably the most dangerous thing we do.
<v Austin>车灯调得很低。音乐阴森森的。每个人都有点紧张，因为你即将要做的事情——可能是我们做过的最危险的事。

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:28.590
<v Soundtrack>Launch control, LV. Go ahead. All video recorders off. Roger.
<v Soundtrack>发射控制，LV，请讲。所有摄像机已关闭。收到。

00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:57.650
<v Soundtrack>At this point he flips on his emergency lights and starts speeding up a little bit up the hill, turns the car around. Lets the car roll down the hill, accelerating all the way. His lights are turning on and off, on and off, on and off. Driving very, very quickly, at this point, around turns.
<v Soundtrack>这时，他打开应急灯，开始在山坡上加速，掉转车头。让车顺着山坡滑下，全程加速。他的车灯一闪一闪的。此时，车速飞快地过弯。

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.280
<v Ira Glass>So basically, you're going down a hill?
<v Ira Glass>所以基本上，你们是在下坡？

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:04.050
<v Austin>Yeah. We're going down a very steep, windy hill.
<v Austin>对，我们在一条非常陡峭、弯曲的山路上行驶。

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:08.780
<v Ira Glass>It's remote enough, by the way, that in a year of doing this, they have never even seen another car.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一下，这里很偏僻，做这件事的一年里，他们甚至从没见过另一辆车。

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:14.510
<v Ira Glass>None of this would be fun during the daytime, Austin says. At night, nobody sees you. You can do anything.
<v Ira Glass>奥斯汀说，白天做这些一点意思都没有。晚上没人看见你，你可以做任何事。

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:22.390
<v Austin>Late at night is kind of the whole fun of the event. People don't really pay attention to who's doing what at night.
<v Austin>深夜正是活动的乐趣所在。人们晚上根本不会注意是谁在做什么。

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.580
<v Austin>[SINGING]
<v Austin>[歌唱]

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:32.910
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》，由公共广播国际公司发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:03:32.910 --> 00:04:05.400
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, we spend an hour with the secret society that is up in the middle of the night. All the people doing things that they would never do during the daytime, and the people doing things that they might be during the day that just feel different when you do them at night. Moving products, and hauling across a war zone, and caring for sick kids, and just wandering the streets to get out of the house. Everything is different at night. We witness how in five acts. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天，我们节目将花一个小时走进深夜里活跃的秘密社会。所有那些人们在白天绝不会做的事情，以及人们在白天做同样的事情但晚上做感觉却完全不同的事情。运输货物、穿越战区、照顾生病的孩子，或者仅仅是出门四处游荡以逃离家门。到了晚上，一切都变得不同。我们将通过五个篇章见证这一点。请继续收听。

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:09.220
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Orange You Glad I Didn't Say Banana?
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：我没说香蕉你高兴吗？

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:25.930
<v Ira Glass>To get to the Hunts Point Market, you drive through this industrial part of the South Bronx, and at night, it's abandoned. For miles, you pass all these closed auto repair lots and plumbing supply stores. And then you go through a fence, pass some pretty intense security, drive a bit more, and suddenly, boom. You are in the Hunts Point Terminal Market.
<v Ira Glass>要到亨茨角市场，你需要穿过南布朗克斯的工业区，晚上那里是一片荒凉。你经过数英里关闭的汽车修理厂和管道用品店，然后穿过一道围栏，经过严格的安检，再往前开一点，突然，砰！你就在亨茨角批发市场里了。

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:36.730
<v Ira Glass>And it's like a busy city at rush hour. Bright and loud. Forklifts whipping around, carrying pallets to trucks. Warehouses in long rows, several city blocks long. 60 acres of produce.
<v Ira Glass>这里就像繁忙城市的早高峰一样，明亮而喧闹。叉车飞快地穿梭，运送着托盘到卡车上。仓库排列成行，有好几个街区那么长。60英亩的农产品。

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:43.410
<v Ira Glass>When people arrive here at 10 PM to work, what they say to each other is "good morning," because the middle of the night is when the most of the business gets done.
<v Ira Glass>当人们在晚上10点来到这里工作时，他们互相打招呼的方式是“早上好”，因为大部分生意都是在深夜完成的。

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:58.470
<v Ira Glass>Two members of the team of economics reporters at Planet Money, Adam Davidson and Chana Joffe-Walt usually report on financial markets and derivative markets and currency markets, so they were pleased to get a chance to actually visit a market what people buy and sell stuff that you can see and touch and smell. Here they are.
<v Ira Glass>Planet Money经济记者团队的两名成员，亚当·戴维森和查娜·乔夫-沃尔特，通常报道金融市场、衍生品市场和货币市场，所以他们很高兴有机会参观一个人们买卖看得见、摸得着、闻得到的真实商品市场。他们来了。

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:00.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Eddie One-Way needs some pears.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>埃迪·单行道需要一些梨。

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:10.730
<v Adam Davidson>His name is actually Edward Joseph. They call him "Eddie One-Way" because he wants prices to go one way-- down. He's a buyer, one of thousands of guys who show up here every night.
<v Adam Davidson>他的真名叫爱德华·约瑟夫。大家叫他“埃迪·单行道”，因为他希望价格只往一个方向走——下跌。他是买家，是每晚出现在这里的成千上万个人中的一员。

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:24.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And they're buying for pretty much every grocery store, restaurant, corner market in the northeast. If you have ever bought a Golden Delicious apple or ate a salad in a restaurant between Philly and Boston, chances are it came through here.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们为东北部的每一家杂货店、餐馆、街角小店购买货物。如果你曾在费城和波士顿之间买过金冠苹果或在餐厅吃过沙拉，很可能它就是从这里流出的。

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:33.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And it is nothing like when you or I buy a salad at a restaurant. There are no posted prices. It us pure supply and demand.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这和你我在餐厅买沙拉完全不同。这里没有标价，纯粹是供需关系。

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:37.950
<v Adam Davidson>And right now, demand-- that's Eddie One-Way, the buyer-- is looking for supply.
<v Adam Davidson>现在，需求方——就是买家埃迪·单行道——正在寻找供应。

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:42.580
<v Eddie One-way>Let's see here, where's my salesman? Oh, Mr. Timmy?
<v Eddie One-way>让我看看，我的推销员在哪？哦，提米先生？

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.500
<v Adam Davidson>Timmy is a salesman at one of the wholesalers here, to Rigo Brothers.
<v Adam Davidson>提米是这里的一家批发商Rigo兄弟的推销员。

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:50.820
<v Eddie One-way>He's close by. I think he's in one of these refrigerators.
<v Eddie One-way>他就在附近，我想他在其中一个冷库里。

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:57.210
<v Eddie One-way>This place here is a stadium. I think you need GPS to get around here. It would help.
<v Eddie One-way>这个地方就像个体育场，我觉得你需要GPS才能转得开。这会有帮助。

00:05:57.210 --> 00:06:07.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Eventually, Eddie One-Way finds Timmy, and they enter into a ritual that will happen thousands of times throughout the night. They try to negotiate a price.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>最终，埃迪找到了提米，他们开始了一场整晚会上演成千上万次的仪式。他们试图讨价还价。

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:12.070
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Eddie thinks $15 for a box of pears seems fair. Timmy doesn't.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>埃迪觉得一箱梨15美元很合理，但提米不这么认为。

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.000
<v Timmy>No way.
<v Timmy>没门。

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:14.270
<v Eddie One-way>We could do it--
<v Eddie One-way>我们可以——

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.100
<v Timmy>I told you 20 yesterday.
<v Timmy>我昨天跟你说了是20。

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:20.780
<v Eddie One-way>Yesterday's gone. Today's a new day.
<v Eddie One-way>昨天已经过去了，今天又是新的一天。

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:23.480
<v Timmy>$20 yesterday, 18 today. That's it.
<v Timmy>昨天20，今天18，就这价。

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:24.840
<v Eddie One-way>Stay close, Timmy.
<v Eddie One-way>留步，提米。

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.980
<v Adam Davidson>Timmy offers Eddie a different kind of pear for $15, which to me looked exactly the same.
<v Adam Davidson>提米给了埃迪另一种梨，要价15美元，在我看来它们长得一模一样。

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.450
<v Eddie One-way>$15 on these, $18 on them.
<v Eddie One-way>这些15美元，那些18美元。

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:38.310
<v Adam Davidson>But Eddie One-Way knows things about pears that I will never know, and he ignores that ridiculous offer.
<v Adam Davidson>但埃迪·单行道对梨的了解是我永远无法企及的，他无视了这个荒谬的报价。

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.720
<v Adam Davidson>Why do you want to pay $15? Why is 18--?
<v Adam Davidson>为什么你要付15？为什么18就——？

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.080
<v Eddie One-way>I'd like to pay 13, but I'm being generous.
<v Eddie One-way>我想付13，但我已经在慷慨解囊了。

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:52.110
<v Timmy>All right. That's it, brother. That's it, man. 18 is the right number. You know it's what they're worth.
<v Timmy>行了，兄弟，就这价，伙计。18是公道价，你知道它们就值这么多。

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.980
<v Eddie One-way>The night's still young.
<v Eddie One-way>夜还长着呢。

00:06:53.980 --> 00:07:17.980
<v Adam Davidson>Eventually Eddie One-Way walks away. The night is still young. At this hour the sellers, guys like Timmy, feel like they are in control. They don't want to give anything away too cheap yet. There's lots more buyers still to come. But Eddie, the buyer, is hoping that if he comes back later in the night, Timmy won't have sold that much. He'll be desperate to get rid of his pears, and will accept a lower price. But Eddie's really taking a risk here.
<v Adam Davidson>最终埃迪走开了。夜还长着呢。在这个钟点，像提米这样的卖家感觉自己掌控着局面。他们不想太早降价，还有更多的买家会来。但买家埃迪希望如果他晚些时候回来，提米还没卖掉那么多货。他会急于清理梨子，从而接受更低的价格。但埃迪确实在冒险。

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:53.950
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I know. I actually saw this risk play out with carrots, and it was really stressful. Jeff Steinberg is a buyer like Eddie, and he was looking for this one particular kind of carrot. A fancy one. Loose California. And I followed him around, walking the aisles, one seller to another. And at first, everyone has the carrots, but Jeff is offended-- or at least acts offended-- by the high prices. And then an hour passes. And now nobody has Loose Californias anymore. Jeff can't find the carrots he needs at any price. So he's thinking he may have to settle for something inferior-- Loose Canadians.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我知道，我亲眼看到这种风险发生在胡萝卜身上，真的让人压力很大。杰夫·斯坦伯格是像埃迪一样的买家，他在寻找一种特殊的胡萝卜，那种散装的加州胡萝卜。我跟着他逛遍了各个通道，从一个卖家到另一个卖家。起初，每个人都有货，但杰夫对高价感到不满——或者至少表现出不满。一小时过去了，现在没人有那种散装加州胡萝卜了。杰夫无论出多少钱都买不到他需要的胡萝卜。他不得不考虑退而求其次——散装加拿大胡萝卜。

00:07:53.950 --> 00:07:54.670
<v Jeff Steinberg>Eugh.
<v Jeff Steinberg>呃。

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:59.980
<v Adam Davidson>Jeff asks a seller named Carlos how much for the Loose Canadians. Carlos tells him eight bucks a box.
<v Adam Davidson>杰夫问一个叫卡洛斯的卖家散装加拿大胡萝卜多少钱。卡洛斯告诉他八块钱一箱。

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:03.180
<v Jeff Steinberg>I'll be right back, then.
<v Jeff Steinberg>那我待会儿再来。

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What's he doing now?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他现在在做什么？

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:08.450
<v Carlos>Going to look at something that he's going to come back and tell me he doesn't like.
<v Carlos>去看看别的东西，然后回来说他不满意。

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.440
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What does it mean that he's not going to like it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他说他不满意是什么意思？

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:21.770
<v Carlos>Probably because they're not good enough for his customers. He's fairly picky, so you have to give the right people the right stuff.
<v Carlos>可能是因为质量达不到他客户的要求。他很挑剔，所以你必须给对的人提供对的东西。

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:24.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And sure enough, Jeff comes out of the warehouse, shaking his head.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>果不其然，杰夫走出仓库，摇着头。

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:25.990
<v Jeff Steinberg>No good.
<v Jeff Steinberg>不行。

00:08:25.990 --> 00:08:29.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No good? And where are you going to look for carrots next?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>不行？那你接下来打算去哪找胡萝卜？

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:33.040
<v Jeff Steinberg>That's a good question, because I've pretty much looked all over the place.
<v Jeff Steinberg>问得好，因为我基本上已经把所有地方都找遍了。

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.040
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So it's 11:30 at night, and carrots are your problem at the moment.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在是晚上11:30，胡萝卜成了你目前的问题。

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:58.100
<v Jeff Steinberg>At the moment. It's coming down to that hour where I'm probably going to have to pay what they wanted, what I don't want to pay.
<v Jeff Steinberg>目前是这样。快到那个钟点了，我可能不得不支付他们想要的价格，即使我不愿意。

00:08:58.100 --> 00:09:11.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Around midnight, it starts to get hectic. Buyers fill the streets. Sellers wave their arms and yell into faces and cell phones. There's a lot of sweating. And as the minutes pass, all these little stories emerge.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>午夜左右，市场开始变得混乱。街道挤满了买家。卖家挥舞着手臂，对着人脸和手机大喊大叫。到处都是汗水。随着时间流逝，各种小故事层出不穷。

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:19.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Midnight, the big story-- tomatoes. They're on fire. Yesterday they were selling for $15. Now they're $25. Everyone is buying.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>午夜，大新闻是西红柿。火得不行。昨天还是15美元，现在涨到25美元。所有人都在抢购。

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:29.060
<v Adam Davidson>Also breaking this hour-- leafy greens from California. They're huge, because as everyone says, they have weather out there. There's not enough leafy greens coming to satisfy the demand.
<v Adam Davidson>同样火爆的还有加州的绿叶蔬菜。需求量极大，正如大家所说，因为那边天气好。绿叶蔬菜的供应满足不了需求。

00:09:29.060 --> 00:09:41.950
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's around this time that we meet Angela Poricelli. Angela is the only woman we see the entire night. She says she knows of only one other woman who works here alongside something like 7,000 men.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>大约在这个时候，我们遇到了安吉拉·波里切利。安吉拉是我们整晚看到的唯一一名女性。她说她只知道这里有另一位女性，与大约7000名男性在这里工作。

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:46.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Her booth is decorated with pictures. She has a big cross. She brings in fresh flowers every week.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她的摊位装饰着照片，挂着一个大十字架。她每周都会带新鲜花束来。

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:58.900
<v Adam Davidson>She offered me candy. And hanging out with her, we learned that it's not all business here in the middle of the night. We keep seeing her next door neighbor, Henry Polio, stopping by on all sorts of dubious missions. Right now he's looking for a fork.
<v Adam Davidson>她请我吃了糖果。和她在一起，我们了解到在深夜这里不仅仅是生意。我们一直看到她的邻居亨利·波利奥过来，进行各种可疑的任务。现在他在找一把叉子。

00:09:58.900 --> 00:09:59.810
<v Angela Poricelli>Hello.
<v Angela Poricelli>你好。

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:00.720
<v Henry Polio>Do you have a fork?
<v Henry Polio>你有叉子吗？

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:01.630
<v Angela Poricelli>Yes, I do.
<v Angela Poricelli>有。

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:15.510
<v Henry Polio>She's the loveliest woman in the market. Believe me, she's the most wonderful person in the world. I know  who her family is. Our family, grandfathers know grandfathers, fathers knew fathers. Wonderful woman.
<v Henry Polio>她是市场上最可爱的女人。相信我，她是世界上最棒的人。我知道她的家人是谁。我们的祖父认识祖父，父亲认识父亲。了不起的女人。

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:16.130
<v Angela Poricelli>Thank you, Henry.
<v Angela Poricelli>谢谢你，亨利。

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:16.450
<v Henry Polio>All right.
<v Henry Polio>好。

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:21.750
<v Angela Poricelli>Bye.
<v Angela Poricelli>再见。

00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:35.600
<v Adam Davidson>When you buy a few hundred pounds of fruit or vegetables, there is so much you don't know. What's the quality? How long has it been on the truck? Is the price reasonable? And it's true-- sellers and buyers who don't know each other that well, they lie to each other all the time.
<v Adam Davidson>当你购买几百磅水果或蔬菜时，有很多你是不知道的。质量如何？在卡车上待了多久？价格合理吗？确实如此——那些不太熟悉的卖家和买家，他们一直在互相撒谎。

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:42.570
<v Adam Davidson>So with all this uncertainty, there is tremendous economic value in long-term, trusting relationships.
<v Adam Davidson>所以，在所有这些不确定性中，长期且相互信任的关系具有巨大的经济价值。

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:46.830
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Hey Adam? You're overthinking it it's so much simpler than that.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嘿，亚当？你想得太多了，事情远比那简单。

00:10:46.830 --> 00:10:49.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I followed Henry from Angela's back to his booth.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我跟着亨利从安吉拉那回到他的摊位。

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:50.840
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You kind of have a crush on Angela?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你是不是对安吉拉有点好感？

00:10:50.840 --> 00:10:54.410
<v Henry Polio>No, I do, since I'm a kid. But she always said no.
<v Henry Polio>不，我是，从我小时候就是。但她总是拒绝。

00:10:54.410 --> 00:10:54.800
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Really?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>真的吗？

00:10:54.800 --> 00:10:59.500
<v Henry Polio>Yeah. I want to take her upstairs and she don't to.
<v Henry Polio>是啊。我想带她去楼上，但她不肯。

00:10:59.500 --> 00:11:00.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Have you ever asked her out?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你约过她出去吗？

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.030
<v Henry Polio>No, no, no.
<v Henry Polio>没有，没有。

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:03.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Does she know that you're interested?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她知道你对她有意思吗？

00:11:03.230 --> 00:11:07.880
<v Henry Polio>Oh, she knows. I tell her.
<v Henry Polio>哦，她知道。我告诉过她。

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:11.140
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You've worked next door to her for all these years and you're never been out with her?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你在她隔壁工作了这么多年，却从未和她出去过？

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:13.670
<v Henry Polio>No! And I ain't going to.
<v Henry Polio>没有！我永远也不会。

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:14.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Why?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>为什么？

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:18.030
<v Henry Polio>Because It's not the right thing to do. She's my sister.
<v Henry Polio>因为那是不对的。她是我的姐妹。

00:11:18.030 --> 00:11:19.100
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>She's not your sister.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她不是你姐妹。

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:22.310
<v Henry Polio>Well, she's close to it.
<v Henry Polio>嗯，差不多了。

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:24.050
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You're not with anybody.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你现在有伴吗？

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.990
<v Henry Polio>Who, me? Yeah, I got a wife.
<v Henry Polio>我？我有老婆。

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:38.800
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Actually, I followed up. Henry doesn't have a wife. He did, but his marriage fell apart. And he blames the night shift for that. They never saw each other. He says all these guys here have wives they never see, kids never see.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>事实上，我追问了一下。亨利没有老婆。他曾有过，但婚姻破裂了。他把这归咎于夜班。他们从不见面。他说这里的这些人都有着几乎不见面的老婆，孩子也从不见面。

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:47.970
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So I go back to Angela's, and turns out she's also divorced. She raised two boys on her own. And she says, yeah. The night shift definitely gets in the way of life.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>于是我回到安吉拉那儿，结果发现她也离婚了。她独自抚养两个男孩。她说，是的，夜班确实阻碍了生活。

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:59.280
<v Angela Poricelli>I remember my father. There's a lot of things he couldn't make it. On Sundays, if there was a wedding, he couldn't go. Sundays, work. My graduation, too, was on a Friday, and he couldn't come.
<v Angela Poricelli>我还记得我的父亲。有很多事情他都错过了。周日如果有婚礼，他去不了。周日要工作。我的毕业典礼在周五，他也来不了。

00:11:59.280 --> 00:11:59.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Really?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>真的吗？

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.710
<v Angela Poricelli>Yeah. But that's OK. I understood.
<v Angela Poricelli>是的。但没关系，我理解。

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:08.080
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So Henry, he said you'll never go out with them because you think of him as a brother.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那么亨利说你永远不会和他出去是因为你把他当哥哥。

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:17.710
<v Angela Poricelli>Yes. I agree with him there. That I do. Plus, I'm engaged to someone right now. His name is Wayne. He's a Green Bay Packer fan.
<v Angela Poricelli>是的，我同意这一点。确实如此。而且，我现在订婚了。他叫韦恩，是绿湾包装工队的球迷。

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:18.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Does he work in the market?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他在市场工作吗？

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:19.410
<v Angela Poricelli>No.
<v Angela Poricelli>不。

00:12:19.410 --> 00:12:20.850
<v Adam Davidson>Does he work days?
<v Adam Davidson>他上白班吗？

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:22.030
<v Angela Poricelli>Days, yes.
<v Angela Poricelli>是的，白班。

00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:24.450
<v Adam Davidson>How is it? It's hard to start a relationship.
<v Adam Davidson>感觉怎么样？开始一段关系很难吧。

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:39.650
<v Angela Poricelli>Very hard, very hard. It's just Friday and Saturday, really, that we see each other.
<v Angela Poricelli>非常难，非常难。真的只有周五和周六我们能见面。

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:40.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Can I check back in with you?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我可以以后再找你聊吗？

00:12:40.360 --> 00:12:42.140
<v Henry Polio>Yeah, go ahead.
<v Henry Polio>可以，没问题。

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:46.120
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's one o'clock in the morning, and I'm with Henry again. He's taking a quick break.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>凌晨一点，我又和亨利在一起。他正忙里偷闲。

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:49.110
<v Henry Polio>I ordered lunch, I've got Guido here, and that makes tonight.
<v Henry Polio>我点了午饭，我有圭多在这里，这就是今晚。

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:51.450
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's one o'clock in the morning and you just ordered lunch.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>凌晨一点，你刚点了午饭。

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:57.500
<v Henry Polio>Yes, yes. So everything is going smooth. Tomatoes are on fire.
<v Henry Polio>是的。所以一切进展顺利。西红柿很火。

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:57.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Still?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>还是吗？

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:01.300
<v Henry Polio>Yeah. We're sold out and they didn't come off the truck.
<v Henry Polio>是啊。我们卖光了，而且货都没下卡车。

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:16.020
<v Adam Davidson>This is the point in the night where the market really changes. There are none of those people buying fancy stuff for high-end restaurants and specialty Manhattan stores. They're all gone. There's no more high-end guys like Jeff inspecting every carrot.
<v Adam Davidson>这是市场真正发生转变的时刻。那些为高档餐厅和曼哈顿特色商店购买高档货的人都不见了。像杰夫那种检查每一根胡萝卜的高端买家也不在了。

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:30.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A whole new group of buyers enters the market now. And they're buying for not-so-fancy places-- bodegas and grocery stores in working class neighborhoods, pushcart vendors. They're not quite as concerned with getting the best possible quality. They want a good price.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在一大群新买家进入市场。他们为那些不那么高档的地方采购——工人社区的小商店、杂货店和流动小贩。他们不像之前那样关注极致的质量，他们想要好价格。

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:48.640
<v Adam Davidson>It's in this second phase that the dynamic between sellers and buyers starts to shift. Remember early in the night, when Eddie One-Way had to go searching for someone to sell him pears? Over the next couple hours, it's the reverse. The sellers are looking for the buyers. And this, says a warehouse man named Thomas Kilgaren--
<v Adam Davidson>在第二阶段，买卖双方的动态开始转变。记得夜里早些时候，埃迪·单行道必须四处寻找卖家买梨吗？接下来的几个小时则是反过来的。卖家开始寻找买家。这是仓库员工托马斯·基尔加伦所说的——

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Nickname Killer--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>外号“杀手”——

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.980
<v Adam Davidson>Is the time to see who is a great salesman and who's not.
<v Adam Davidson>是时候看看谁是好推销员，谁不是了。

00:13:53.980 --> 00:14:13.280
<v Thomas Kilgaren>There's some guys down here that couldn't sell life insurance to the Kennedys. Know what I mean? But there's some that are pretty good. If you could sell something that somebody doesn't want, that's a good salesman. I come down here looking for lettuce, you sell me lettuce-- any moron could do that. Sell me something that I don't want-- you know what? That's a salesman.
<v Thomas Kilgaren>有些人在这根本卖不掉任何东西。你知道我的意思吧？但有些人很厉害。如果你能把别人不需要的东西卖出去，那才是好推销员。我来这找生菜，你卖我生菜——任何笨蛋都能做到。卖我一些我根本不需要的东西——你知道吗？那才是销售。

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:22.600
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Selling something that no one wants? That is exactly what a guy named Mike Sack, Big Mike, is trying to do right now. We catch up with him toward the end of the night.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>卖出别人不需要的东西？这正是迈克·萨克（大迈克）现在试图做的事。我们在夜色临近尾声时赶上了他。

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:50.070
<v Adam Davidson>Earlier in the night, buyers were begging Big Mike to lower his prices, and he didn't. He refused. And it worked out pretty well for him. He sold a lot of Red Globe grapes and mangoes, and he made a killing in persimmons. But now that daylight's approaching, he's got piles and piles of oranges from Chile, and he's the one begging. He's pleading with one of his regular carports, a guy named Amerigo Perrera, to buy some of those oranges. Amerigo is pretty clear about his wishes.
<v Adam Davidson>夜里早些时候，买家曾求着大迈克降价，他没降。他拒绝了。这对他很有效，他卖出了很多红地球葡萄和芒果，在柿子上也大赚了一笔。但随着黎明临近，他还有成堆来自智利的橙子，现在轮到他求人了。他乞求他的常客阿梅里戈·佩雷拉买点橙子。阿梅里戈的意愿很明确。

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:50.640
<v Amerigo Perrera>No oranges.
<v Amerigo Perrera>不要橙子。

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:52.860
<v Big Mike>You've got to give in, Amerigo, today. You've got to be nice.
<v Big Mike>阿梅里戈，今天你得让步，得通情达理点。

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.370
<v Amerigo Perrera>What part of no oranges don't you understand?
<v Amerigo Perrera>你听不懂什么叫不要橙子吗？

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:56.640
<v Big Mike>I'll give you one pallet.
<v Big Mike>我给你一托盘。

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:57.510
<v Amerigo Perrera>No! Stop playing.
<v Amerigo Perrera>不！别闹了。

00:14:57.510 --> 00:15:01.310
<v Big Mike>Be nice today. Be nice in front of the camera. There's a lady present.
<v Big Mike>今天客气点。在镜头前表现好点，有女士在场。

00:15:01.310 --> 00:15:03.510
<v Amerigo Perrera>Shut up already.
<v Amerigo Perrera>闭嘴吧。

00:15:03.510 --> 00:15:04.690
<v Big Mike>Can I give you one for $18?
<v Big Mike>我能给你一箱18美元吗？

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:05.500
<v Amerigo Perrera>No, Mike, no.
<v Amerigo Perrera>不，迈克，不要。

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:06.220
<v Big Mike>Just be nice.
<v Big Mike>客气点。

00:15:06.220 --> 00:15:06.480
<v Amerigo Perrera>No.
<v Amerigo Perrera>不要。

00:15:06.480 --> 00:15:07.000
<v Big Mike>Why?
<v Big Mike>为什么？

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:07.760
<v Amerigo Perrera>I don't need it today.
<v Amerigo Perrera>我今天不需要。

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:09.490
<v Big Mike>One skid only.
<v Big Mike>就一托盘。

00:15:09.490 --> 00:15:14.710
<v Amerigo Perrera>Stop it, stop it, stop it. This stuff is being recorded, you understand?
<v Amerigo Perrera>住手，别再闹了。东西正在录制，你懂吗？

00:15:14.710 --> 00:15:23.890
<v Big Mike>Yeah, but they just want to see, you know, and I've give you one skid. Be nice for the microphone, Amerigo. Amerigo, one skid. How about a 56.
<v Big Mike>是啊，但他们只是想看，我给你一托盘。对着麦克风表现好点，阿梅里戈。阿梅里戈，一托盘，56怎么样？

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:25.770
<v Amerigo Perrera>Why don't you stop playing around already? No.
<v Amerigo Perrera>你能不能别再闹了？不。

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:28.910
<v Big Mike>How about a 56? Amerigo!
<v Big Mike>56怎么样？阿梅里戈！

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:31.130
<v Amerigo Perrera>No!
<v Amerigo Perrera>不！

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:43.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>At around 4 AM, all of the sellers in the market find themselves pretty much in the same situation as Big Mike. Tired and with too many oranges. Listen to Henry-- so tired.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>凌晨4点左右，市场里的所有卖家发现自己和迈克处于同样的境地。疲惫不堪，橙子堆积如山。听听亨利——太累了。

00:15:43.480 --> 00:15:55.410
<v Henry Polio>Oranges. Oranges are moving slow, so you know, you try to give everybody some oranges. Even if they don't want it, they're taking them. You know what I mean? Poor couple. This is, like, what time do you go to bed at night?
<v Henry Polio>橙子。橙子卖得慢，所以你得试着塞给每个人一些橙子。即使他们不想要，也得收下。你知道我的意思吧？这对可怜的夫妇。这是，你晚上几点睡觉？

00:15:55.410 --> 00:15:58.080
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What time I go to bed? Probably like 11.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我几点睡觉？大概11点吧。

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:05.310
<v Henry Polio>So this is like your nine o'clock. You know? I hate the day.
<v Henry Polio>所以这就好比你的九点钟。你知道吗？我讨厌白天。

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:06.260
<v Adam Davidson>Wait, you hate the day?
<v Adam Davidson>等等，你讨厌白天？

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:07.590
<v Henry Polio>Oh, I hate it.
<v Henry Polio>哦，我讨厌。

00:16:07.590 --> 00:16:08.800
<v Adam Davidson>What do you hate about the day?
<v Adam Davidson>你讨厌白天的什么？

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:33.630
<v Henry Polio>I don't know. The sun, everything. I get a headache. How are you, my friend? I just don't like the sun. I don't like it. I mean, I like it, but you know, like now, if the sun came up, I hate it. The morning, I ain't into. I like the dark.
<v Henry Polio>我不知道。太阳，所有一切。我头疼。你好吗，朋友？我就是不喜欢太阳。我不喜欢它。我是说，我喜欢它，但你看现在，如果太阳升起来，我就讨厌。早晨，我不喜欢。我喜欢黑暗。

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:40.610
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>At 5 AM, Henry heads home. He rakes some leaves in the front yard, and then heads to sleep just as the sun comes out.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>清晨5点，亨利回家了。他在前院耙了一些落叶，然后在阳光出来时上床睡觉。

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:50.780
<v Adam Davidson>And later that day, a fancy grocery store in Greenwich, Connecticut might have some very nice, but kind of pricey, tomatoes. A high-end restaurant in Manhattan may be offering the chef's tomato bisque.
<v Adam Davidson>那天晚些时候，康涅狄格州格林威治的一家高档杂货店可能会有非常不错但价格昂贵的西红柿。曼哈顿的一家高档餐厅可能会提供主厨特制西红柿浓汤。

00:16:50.780 --> 00:17:07.040
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But further out, in poorer neighborhoods in South Boston, Brooklyn, Queens, tomatoes will be very hard to come by. Oranges, though, will be very cheap. And there will be lots of them.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但在南波士顿、布鲁克林、皇后区的贫困社区，西红柿会非常难买到。不过橙子会非常便宜，到处都是。

00:17:07.040 --> 00:17:57.160
<v Ira Glass>Chana Joffe-Walt and Adam Davidson of Planet Money, which is a co-production of our radio show and NPR news. You can hear them three times a week speaking in language anybody can understand about the human drama that's part of all kinds of economic systems at their podcast at www.npr.org/money.
<v Ira Glass>以上是来自Planet Money的查娜·乔夫-沃尔特和亚当·戴维森，这是我们节目与NPR新闻的联合制作。你可以在每周三次的播客中收听他们用通俗易懂的语言讲述各类经济体系背后的真实故事，网址是 www.npr.org/money。

00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:00.270
<v Ira Glass>Act Two. It Was a Dark and Smoky Night.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕：黑暗而烟雾弥漫的夜晚。

00:18:00.270 --> 00:18:08.700
<v Ira Glass>Jenifer Hixson told this story about the random kinds of things that can happen in the middle of the night at The Moth, where people get on stage and tell personal stories in front of an audience.
<v Ira Glass>詹妮弗·希克森在“飞蛾”讲述了这个关于深夜里可能发生的随机事件的故事，“飞蛾”是一个人们上台在观众面前讲述个人故事的节目。

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:28.610
<v Jenifer Hixson>I reached over and secretly undid my seatbelt. And when his foot hit the break at the red light, I flung open the door and I ran. I had no shoes on. I was crying. I had no wallet. But I was OK, because I had my cigarettes.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我悄悄解开安全带。当他在红灯前踩下刹车时，我猛地推开车门跑了。我没穿鞋，在哭，没带钱包。但我没事，因为我有香烟。

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:53.060
<v Jenifer Hixson>When you live with someone who has a temper, a very bad temper, a very, very bad temper, you learn to play around that. You learn, this time I'll play possum, and next time I'll just be real nice, or I'll say yes to everything, or you make yourself scarce, or you run. And this was one of the times when you just run.
<v Jenifer Hixson>当你和一个脾气火爆的人生活在一起时，非常火爆，坏到极点的脾气，你就会学会如何应对。你会学到，这次我装死，下次我就乖巧点，或者对一切都说好，或者让自己消失，或者逃跑。而这是你不得不逃跑的时刻之一。

00:18:53.060 --> 00:19:09.010
<v Jenifer Hixson>And as I was running, I thought, this was a great place to jump out. Because there were big lawns and there were cul-de-sacs, and sometimes he would come after me, and drive, and yell stuff at me to get back in, get back in, and I was like, no. I'm out of here. This is great. And I went and hid behind a cabana and he left.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我在跑的时候想，这里真是跳车的好地方，因为这里有大草坪和死胡同。有时他会开车追我，对我大喊大叫让我上车，而我心想，不，我离开这里了。太棒了。我躲到了一个更衣室后面，他开走了。

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:44.280
<v Jenifer Hixson>And I started to walk in this beautiful neighborhood. It was 10:30 at night. And there was no sound except for sprinklers. Ch, ch, ch, ch, brrrrr. Ch, ch, ch, ch, brrrr. And I was enjoying myself, and enjoying the absence of anger, and enjoying these few hours I knew I'd have of freedom. And just to perfect it, I thought, I'll have a smoke. And then it occurred to me, with horrifying speed, I don't have a light!
<v Jenifer Hixson>我开始在这个美丽的街区走。当时是晚上10:30，除了洒水器的声音外一片寂静。嚓，嚓，嚓，嚓，嗡嗡嗡。嚓，嚓，嚓，嚓，嗡嗡嗡。我很享受，享受愤怒的消散，享受我知道自己能拥有的那几个小时的自由。为了让这一切更完美，我想，抽支烟吧。然后我以极快的速度意识到，我没有火！

00:19:44.280 --> 00:20:02.860
<v Jenifer Hixson>Just then, as if in answer, I see a figure up ahead. Who is that? It's not him, OK. They don't have a dog. Who is that? What are they doing out on this suburban street? And the person comes closer, and I can see it's a woman. And then I can see she has her hands in her face. Oh, she's crying.
<v Jenifer Hixson>就在这时，仿佛回应我，我看到前方有一个人影。那是谁？不是他，没关系。他们没带狗。那是谁？他们在这个郊区的街道上做什么？那个人走近了，我能看出是个女人。然后我能看到她正双手掩面。哦，她在哭。

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:18.060
<v Jenifer Hixson>And then she sees me, and she composes herself, and she gets closer, and I see she has no shoes on. And just as she passes me, she says, you got a cigarette? And I say, you got a light? And she says, damn, I hope so.
<v Jenifer Hixson>接着她看到我，稳住情绪，走近了些。我看到她没穿鞋。就在她经过我身边时，她说，你有烟吗？我说，你有火吗？她说，该死，我希望有。

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:42.870
<v Jenifer Hixson>And then first she digs into our cutoffs in the front-- nothing. And then digs in the back, and then she has this vest on that has 50 million little pockets on it. She's checking and checking, and it's looking bad. She digs back in the front again-- deep, deep-- and she pulls out a pack of matches that have been laundered at least once. We open it up and there is one match inside.
<v Jenifer Hixson>然后她先翻了翻前面的兜——什么都没有。又翻了翻后面，接着她身上穿的那件背心有5000万个小口袋。她翻啊翻，情况不妙。她又翻了翻前面——深深地——掏出一盒至少被洗过一次的火柴。我们打开它，里面只有一根火柴。

00:20:42.870 --> 00:21:01.020
<v Jenifer Hixson>OK. Oh my god. This takes on-- it's like Nassau. Now we've got to like, how are we going to do it? OK. And we hunker down. We crouch on the ground. And where's the wind coming from? We're stopping. I take out my cigarettes. Let's get the cigarettes ready. Oh, my brand, she says. And we both have our cigarettes at the ready.
<v Jenifer Hixson>好吧。天哪。这变得——就像拿骚。现在我们得，我们要怎么做？好。我们蹲下来，蜷缩在地上。风是从哪来的？我们停下来。我拿出香烟。把香烟准备好。哦，我抽的这个牌子，她说。我们俩都准备好了香烟。

00:21:01.020 --> 00:21:15.190
<v Jenifer Hixson>She strikes once, nothing. She strikes again-- yes, fire! Puff, inhale. Mm, sweet kiss of that cigarette. And we sit there, and we're loving the nicotine, and we both need this right now.
<v Jenifer Hixson>她划了一次，没着。又划了一次——着了！抽上一口，吸进去。嗯，那口香烟的甜蜜之吻。我们坐在那儿，沉浸在尼古丁中，我们现在都需要这个。

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:26.400
<v Jenifer Hixson>Immediately we start to reminisce about our 30 second relationship. "I didn't think that was going to happen!" "Me neither!" "Oh, man, that was close!" "Oh, I'm so lucky I saw you!" "Yeah!'
<v Jenifer Hixson>我们立刻开始回忆这30秒的关系。“我没想到会这样！”“我也是！”“天哪，刚才太惊险了！”“我真幸运遇见你！”“是啊！”

00:21:26.400 --> 00:21:39.720
<v Jenifer Hixson>Then she surprises me by saying, "What was the fight about?" And I say, "What are they all about?" And she said, "I know what you mean." She said, "Was it a bad one?" And I said, "You know, like medium." She said, "Oh."
<v Jenifer Hixson>然后她出乎意料地问我：“为什么吵架？”我说：“吵架还能为什么？”她说：“我明白你的意思。”她说：“很严重吗？”我说：“你知道，中等吧。”她说：“哦。”

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:53.220
<v Jenifer Hixson>And we start to trade stories about our lives. We're both from up north. We're both kind of newish to the neighborhood. This is in Florida. We both went to college. Not great colleges, but man, we graduated.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我们开始交换关于我们生活的琐事。我们都来自北方，对这个街区都有点陌生。这是在佛罗里达。我们都上了大学。虽然不是什么名牌大学，但好歹毕业了。

00:21:53.220 --> 00:22:15.240
<v Jenifer Hixson>And I'm actually finding myself a little jealous of her, because she has this really cool job washing dogs. She had horses back home, and she really loved animals, and she wants to be a vet, and I'm like, man, you're halfway there! I'm a waitress at an ice cream parlor. So I don't know where I want to be, but I know it's not that.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我发现自己甚至有点嫉妒她，因为她有一份很酷的工作——帮狗洗澡。她在老家有马，她真的爱动物，想当一名兽医，我心想，伙计，你已经成功一半了！我是冰激凌店的服务员。我不知道我以后想做什么，但我知道肯定不是那份工作。

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:37.100
<v Jenifer Hixson>And then it gets a little deeper, and we share some other stuff about what our lives are like. Things that I can't ever tell people at home. This girl-- I can tell her the really ugly stuff, and she still understands how it can still be pretty. She understands, like, how nice he's going to be when I get home, and how sweet that'll be.
<v Jenifer Hixson>接着话题深入了一些，我们分享了一些关于生活的事情。那些我无法告诉家里人的事情。这个女孩——我可以告诉她那些丑陋的事，她依然能理解事情有时为何还是美好的。她理解，当我回到家时，他会对我多么好，那会是多么甜蜜。

00:22:37.100 --> 00:23:00.930
<v Jenifer Hixson>We are chain smoking off each other. Oh, that's almost out, come on! And we go through this entire pack until it's gone, and then I say, "You know what? This is a little funny, but you're going to have to show me the way to get home. Because although I'm 23 years old, I don't have my driver's license yet, and I just jumped out right when I needed to." And she says, "Well, why don't you come back to my house and I'll give you a ride?" I say, "OK, great," and we start walking.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我们共抽一支烟。哦，快没了，快点！我们把这整盒烟抽完，然后我说：“你知道吗？这有点可笑，但你得带我回家。因为虽然我23岁了，但我还没拿到驾照，而且我跳车跳得正是时候。”她说：“好吧，那你跟我回我家，我开车送你？”我说：“好的，太棒了。”我们开始走。

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:17.570
<v Jenifer Hixson>And we get to this-- lots of lights. And the roads are getting wider and wider, and there's more cars, and I see lots of stores. You know, laundromats, and dollar stores, and EmergiCenters.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我们到达这里——到处是灯光。路越来越宽，车越来越多，我看到了很多商店。你知道，自助洗衣店、一元店、急诊中心。

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:52.440
<v Jenifer Hixson>Then we cross over U.S. 1, and she leads me to someplace, and I think, no! But yes. Carl's Efficiency Apartments. This girl lives there. And it's horrible, and it's lit up so bright, just to illuminate the horribleness of it. It's the kind of place where you drive your car right up, and the door's right there, and there's 50 million cigarette butts outside, and there's like doors one through seven, and you know behind every single door there's some horrible misery going on. There's someone crying or drunk or lonely or cruel. And I think, oh, God. She lives here. How awful.
<v Jenifer Hixson>接着我们穿过美国1号公路，她带我去了某个地方，我心想，别啊！但还是到了。卡尔公寓。这女孩住在那。这里很可怕，灯光亮得刺眼，只是为了照亮那里的恐怖。这地方是你把车直接开到门口，门就在那儿，外面有5000万个烟蒂，有1到7号门，你知道每扇门后都有可怕的苦难在发生。有人在哭，在醉，在孤独或残忍。我想，天哪。她住这儿。真可怕。

00:23:52.440 --> 00:24:07.640
<v Jenifer Hixson>We go to the door, door number four, and she very, very quietly keys in. As soon as the door opens, I hear the blare of television come out, and on the blue light of the television, the smoke of a hundred cigarettes in that little crack of light.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我们来到4号门，她非常、非常安静地开了门。门一打开，电视的吵闹声传来，在电视的蓝光下，那道光缝里弥漫着上百支香烟的烟雾。

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:21.540
<v Jenifer Hixson>And I hear the man, and he says, "Where were you?" And she says," "Never mind, I'm back." And he says, "You all right?" And she says, "Yeah, I'm all right." And then she turns me and says, "You want a beer?" And he says, "Who the [BLEEP] is that?"
<v Jenifer Hixson>我听到那个男人说：“你去哪了？”她说：“别管了，我回来了。”他说：“你没事吧？”她说：“是的，我没事。”接着她转向我说：“想喝啤酒吗？”他问：“那是谁？”

00:24:21.540 --> 00:24:27.710
<v Jenifer Hixson>And she pulls me over, and he sees me, and he says, "Oh, hey." I'm not a threat.
<v Jenifer Hixson>她把我拉过去，他看见我了，说：“哦，嘿。”我不是威胁。

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:57.720
<v Jenifer Hixson>Just then he takes a drag of his cigarette and I follow the cigarette down. And I'm surprised when I see, int he crook of his arm, a little boy, sleeping. A toddler. And I think, [GASP]. And just then, the girl reaches underneath the bed and takes out a carton, and she taps out the last pack of cigarettes in there. And on the way up, she kisses the little boy, and then she kisses the man. And the man says again, "You all right?" And she says, "Yeah. I'm just going to go out and smoke with her."
<v Jenifer Hixson>就在这时，他抽了口烟，我跟着他的视线看向香烟。当我看到他怀里抱着一个小孩在睡觉时，我很惊讶。一个蹒跚学步的孩子。我想，[喘气]。就在这时，女孩从床底下拿出一个纸箱，把里面最后一包香烟拿出来。在她起身时，她亲了亲那个小男孩，然后亲了亲那个男人。男人又问了一遍：“你没事吧？”她说：“是的，我只是要带她出去抽支烟。”

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:13.030
<v Jenifer Hixson>And so we go outside and sit amongst the cigarette butts and smoke. And I say, "Wow, that's your little boy?" And she says, "Yeah, isn't he beautiful?" And I say, "Yeah, he is. He is beautiful." "He's my light. He keeps me going," she says.
<v Jenifer Hixson>于是我们走到外面，坐在烟蒂中间抽烟。我说：“哇，那是你的小男孩吗？”她说：“是啊，他不是很漂亮吗？”我说：“是的，他很漂亮。他是我的光，他支撑着我前进。”她说。

00:25:13.030 --> 00:25:25.390
<v Jenifer Hixson>We finish our cigarettes. She finishes her beer. I don't have a beer, because I can't go home with beer on my breath. And she goes inside to get the keys.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我们抽完了烟。她喝完了啤酒。我没喝啤酒，因为我不能带着酒气回家。她进去拿钥匙。

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:49.750
<v Jenifer Hixson>She takes too long in there getting the keys, and I think something must be wrong. And she comes out and she says, "Look, I'm really sorry. But um, like we don't have any gas in the car, it's already on E, and he needs to get to work in the morning, and um, you know, I'm going to walk to work as it is. So what I did was, though, here, look, I drew out this map for you. And you really, you're like a mile and a half from home, and um, if you walk three streets over, you'll be back on that pretty street, and you just take that, and you'll be fine."
<v Jenifer Hixson>她在里面拿钥匙花的时间太长了，我心想一定出事了。她出来说：“听着，真的很抱歉。但是呢，车里没汽油了，油表指针已经在E档了，他早上还得去上班，我也得走着去上班。不过，给你，看，我为你画了这张地图。你离家其实只有一英里半，只要走过三条街，你就能回到那条漂亮的街道，沿着它走就行了。”

00:25:49.750 --> 00:25:59.600
<v Jenifer Hixson>And she also has wrapped up in toilet paper seven cigarettes for me. A third of her pack, I note. And a new pack of matches.
<v Jenifer Hixson>她还用卫生纸包了七支烟给我。我注意到，那是她那盒烟的三分之一。还有一盒新火柴。

00:25:59.600 --> 00:26:08.220
<v Jenifer Hixson>And she tells me, "Goodbye!", and "That was great to meet you," and "How lucky," and "That was fun," and you know, "Let's be friends."
<v Jenifer Hixson>她跟我说：“再见！很高兴认识你，真幸运，真有趣。”还说“我们做朋友吧。”

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:21.470
<v Jenifer Hixson>And I say, "Yeah, OK!" and I walk away. But I kind of know we're not going to be friends. I might not ever see her again. And I kind of know, I don't think she's ever going to be a vet.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我说：“好的，没问题！”但我心里清楚我们不会成为朋友。我可能再也见不到她了。我也知道，我觉得她永远不会成为一名兽医。

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:33.660
<v Jenifer Hixson>And I cross, and I walk away. And maybe this would have seemed like a visit from my possible future and scary, but it kind of does the opposite. On the walk home I'm like, man. That was really grim over there.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我穿过马路走开。也许这本该看起来像是一个来自我可能的未来且可怕的景象，但实际上恰恰相反。回家的路上我心想，天哪，那边真是太阴郁了。

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:58.950
<v Jenifer Hixson>And I'm going home now to my nice boyfriend, and he's going to be so extra happy to see me. And we have a one-bedroom apartment, and we have two trees, and there's a yard. And we have this jar in the kitchen where there's like loose money that we can use for anything. Like we would never, ever run out of gas. And I don't have a baby, you know? So I can leave whenever I want.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我现在要回家找我那个可爱的男朋友了，他看到我一定会格外高兴。我们有一间一居室的公寓，有两棵树，还有一个院子。厨房里有个罐子，里面有零钱，我们可以用来买任何东西。就像我们永远、永远不会没油一样。而且我没有孩子，所以想什么时候走都可以。

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:19.090
<v Jenifer Hixson>I smoked all seven cigarettes on the way home. And people who have never smoked cigarettes just think ick, disgusting and poison. But unless you've had them, and held them dear, you don't know how great they can be, and what friends and comfort and kinship they can bring.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我在回家的路上抽完了所有七支烟。没抽过烟的人会觉得恶心，是毒药。但除非你曾经抽过，并把香烟视若珍宝，否则你不知道它们有多棒，能带来多少朋友、安慰和亲情。

00:27:19.090 --> 00:27:34.970
<v Jenifer Hixson>It took me a long time to quit that the boyfriend, and then to quit smoking. And sometimes I still miss the smoking.
<v Jenifer Hixson>我花了很长时间才戒掉那个男朋友，然后才戒掉香烟。有时我还是很怀念抽烟的感觉。

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:48.360
<v Ira Glass>Jenifer Hixson at The Moth. You can here more stories like this on The Moth's free weekly podcast at themoth.org. They also have a new weekly radio show, The Moth Radio Hour, that's on many public radio stations around the country.
<v Ira Glass>以上是“飞蛾”节目的詹妮弗·希克森。你可以在 themoth.org 的“飞蛾”免费每周播客中收听更多类似的故事。他们还有一个新的每周广播节目“飞蛾广播小时”，在全国许多公共广播电台播出。

00:27:48.360 --> 00:28:01.720
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, the time of night in Iraq when you can fool yourself into thinking that you're not in Iraq at all. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio and Public Radio International when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下篇，在伊拉克，当你能自欺欺人地认为你根本不在伊拉克的那段夜色时光。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台和公共广播国际的节目继续播出。

00:28:01.720 --> 00:28:12.640
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our show, of course, we choose a theme, bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, The Middle of the Night. Stories that happen when most of us are already asleep.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周我们都会选择一个主题，为你带来关于该主题的各种故事。今天的主题是“深夜”。那些在我们大多数人已经入睡时发生的故事。

00:28:12.640 --> 00:28:18.130
<v Ira Glass>We've arrived at Act Three of our show. Act Three. The Early Bird Catches the Chicken
<v Ira Glass>我们来到了节目的第三幕。第三幕：早起的鸟儿有炸鸡吃。

00:28:18.130 --> 00:28:42.870
<v Ira Glass>Now a story about that people staying up all night hoping for nothing more than a chicken sandwich. When the change Chick-Fil-A opens a new store, they encourage people to line up 24 hours ahead of time, at 6 AM the day before. And for 100 people who do that, Chick-Fil-A gives away coupons for a year of Chick-Fil-A. One combo meal per week. 52 coupons, which is worth, if you add it up, 260 bucks.
<v Ira Glass>现在讲一个关于人们熬夜整晚只为一份炸鸡三明治的故事。每当Chick-Fil-A连锁店开新店时，他们鼓励人们提前24小时，在前一天的早上6点开始排队。对于前100名排队的人，Chick-Fil-A会送出一年的Chick-Fil-A优惠券，每周一份套餐。52张优惠券，算下来价值260美元。

00:28:42.870 --> 00:29:00.280
<v Ira Glass>Well, Dave Hill and Shaina Feinberg were curious about who comes out for that, because a lot of people do. Neither of them is real reporter. Shaina is a very funny writer. Dave is a musician and a comedian. But when a new Chick-Fil-A store opened in Orlando, Florida recently, they went down to stay up all night and see what happens.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫·希尔和沙娜·费恩伯格很好奇谁会来参加，因为确实有很多人来。他们俩都不是正式记者。沙娜是一位非常幽默的作家，戴夫是一位音乐家和喜剧演员。但最近佛罗里达州奥兰多开了一家新的Chick-Fil-A时，他们亲自去熬了个通宵，看看会发生什么。

00:29:00.280 --> 00:29:12.160
<v Dave Hill>OK. It's about nine o'clock. Shaina's trying to set up the tent right now. I would totally help her, but I'm busy reporting. How's it going, Shaina?
<v Dave Hill>好了，大约九点钟了。沙娜正在努力搭帐篷。我本可以帮她，但我正忙着报道。进展如何，沙娜？

00:29:12.160 --> 00:29:21.400
<v Shaina Feinberg>Good.  I think we put this into here, and then we can-- this is where I don't get-- how does this bend?
<v Shaina Feinberg>不错，我觉得这个要塞进这里，然后我们就能——这就是我不明白的地方——这东西怎么弯？

00:29:21.400 --> 00:29:29.160
<v Dave Hill>Again, I would help, if I could, but I can't. I'm busy reporting. Can we interview you?
<v Dave Hill>再一次，如果可以的话我会帮忙，但我不能，我正忙着报道。我们可以采访你吗？

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:30.650
<v Man 1>What do you want to know about?
<v Man 1>你想知道什么？

00:29:30.650 --> 00:29:32.000
<v Dave Hill>Do you know how to set up tents?
<v Dave Hill>你知道怎么搭帐篷吗？

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.290
<v Man 1>Yeah, actually I set that tent up.
<v Man 1>是的，其实那个帐篷就是我搭的。

00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:35.740
<v Dave Hill>Do you know how to set up our tent?
<v Dave Hill>你知道怎么搭我们的帐篷吗？

00:29:35.740 --> 00:29:37.220
<v Man 1>Yeah, I can set up your tent.
<v Man 1>是的，我可以帮你搭。

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:41.480
<v Dave Hill>That'd be awesome.
<v Dave Hill>那太棒了。

00:29:41.480 --> 00:29:54.320
<v Man 2>The DJ will be shutting down about 11:30. At that time, most of the folks will sleep, and at roughly 4:30 in the morning, the DJ comes back, and we wake them up.
<v Man 2>DJ将在11:30左右停止工作。那时，大多数人会睡觉，大约凌晨4:30，DJ回来，我们会把他们叫醒。

00:29:54.320 --> 00:30:09.300
<v Woman 1>It becomes a family-friendly event. Especially in the summer, you'll see whole families out here. So you'll see a whole list of rules about, you know, no drinking, and certain behavior. There's lots of dance contests.
<v Woman 1>这成了家庭友好的活动。特别是在夏天，你会看到整个家庭都来这。所以你会看到一长串规则，比如不准喝酒，某些行为准则。还有很多舞蹈比赛。

00:30:09.300 --> 00:30:22.740
<v Dave Hill>It's 11:23. We're sitting here in the parking lot across from Chick-Fil-A, just to see it from afar, I guess. I'm going to sit closer to you, if you're comfortable with that.
<v Dave Hill>现在是11:23。我们正坐在Chick-Fil-A对面的停车场里，我想大概是为了远距离观察吧。如果你觉得舒服的话，我要坐得离你近点。

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:26.900
<v Shaina Feinberg>Yeah, that's fine. I think we should describe what we see.
<v Shaina Feinberg>嗯，没问题。我觉得我们应该描述一下我们看到的景象。

00:30:26.900 --> 00:30:40.470
<v Dave Hill>Well, we're pretty much in like a regular strip mall parking lot. There's a Blockbuster. There's that that rib place that we ate earlier that I'm kind of regretting.
<v Dave Hill>好吧，我们基本上是在一个普通的商场停车场里。这有个百视达，还有我们之前吃的那个肋排店，我有点后悔吃了。

00:30:40.470 --> 00:30:44.150
<v Shaina Feinberg>Yeah. And basically, there's a lot of tents up.
<v Shaina Feinberg>是啊。基本上，有很多帐篷。

00:30:44.150 --> 00:30:52.170
<v Dave Hill>Easily the most tents I've ever seen in a fast food restaurant parking lot in my lifetime.
<v Dave Hill>这绝对是我这辈子在快餐店停车场见过的最多的帐篷。

00:30:52.170 --> 00:30:57.350
<v Dave Hill>It's weird with radio. You've got to fill in the blanks. The visuals.
<v Dave Hill>广播就是这样，你得填补空白，描述视觉画面。

00:30:57.350 --> 00:31:02.050
<v Dave Hill>We should we should go get some celery and break it, like old time radio.
<v Dave Hill>我们应该去买点芹菜，弄碎它，就像老派广播那样。

00:31:02.050 --> 00:31:03.910
<v Shaina Feinberg>So people will be like, what is that noise?
<v Shaina Feinberg>这样人们就会问，那是什么声音？

00:31:03.910 --> 00:31:13.340
<v Dave Hill>Oh. I just broke my arm in two for no apparent reason.
<v Dave Hill>哦，我刚无缘无故把手臂弄断了。

00:31:13.340 --> 00:31:18.230
<v Dave Hill>Hello? Hello? Hi. I'm Dave.
<v Dave Hill>喂？喂？你好，我是戴夫。

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:20.340
<v Ken Perkins>I'm Ken Perkins.
<v Ken Perkins>我是肯·珀金斯。

00:31:20.340 --> 00:31:21.410
<v Ann>I'm Ann.
<v Ann>我是安。

00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:23.140
<v Ken Perkins>But I call her Hanusha.
<v Ken Perkins>但我叫她哈努莎。

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:23.410
<v Dave Hill>Hanusha?
<v Dave Hill>哈努莎？

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:26.270
<v Ann>Hanusha, in Ukrainian, is Ann.
<v Ann>哈努莎在乌克兰语里就是安的意思。

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:27.740
<v Dave Hill>Oh, I like that.
<v Dave Hill>哦，我喜欢。

00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:29.780
<v Shaina Feinberg>How long have you guys been married?
<v Shaina Feinberg>你们结婚多久了？

00:31:29.780 --> 00:31:30.990
<v Ken Perkins>We aren't married.
<v Ken Perkins>我们没结婚。

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:40.520
<v Ann>We were married to somebody else. But he died, and she died. I think he gave her mushrooms or something.
<v Ann>我们之前都结过婚。但他死了，她也死了。我想是他给她吃了蘑菇之类的。

00:31:40.520 --> 00:31:40.990
<v Ken Perkins>No!
<v Ken Perkins>不！

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:42.290
<v Ann>Poison mushrooms.
<v Ann>毒蘑菇。

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:47.500
<v Ken Perkins>No. My wife died of Alzheimer's just a little over a year ago. And then and her husband died two years ago.
<v Ken Perkins>不。我妻子死于阿兹海默症，刚过去一年多。然后，她的丈夫两年前去世了。

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:48.430
<v Ann>Three.
<v Ann>三年前。

00:31:48.430 --> 00:31:52.030
<v Ken Perkins>She was lonesome, and I was lonesome, so we got our lonesomes together.
<v Ken Perkins>她很寂寞，我也很寂寞，所以我们就凑到一起了。

00:31:52.030 --> 00:31:54.790
<v Shaina Feinberg>Was it love at first sight for you guys?
<v Shaina Feinberg>你们是一见钟情吗？

00:31:54.790 --> 00:31:56.180
<v Ann>For me it was. But he didn't--
<v Ann>对我来说是。但他没——

00:31:56.180 --> 00:31:59.320
<v Ken Perkins>Not for me, not for me. I learned to love her. It took me a while.
<v Ken Perkins>不是我，不是我。我学会去爱她。花了我一阵子。

00:31:59.320 --> 00:32:03.320
<v Dave Hill>Why, why? This one, I'm going wild for this one, and I've only been been here like three minutes.
<v Dave Hill>为什么，为什么？这个，我对这一对真的非常感兴趣，虽然我只在这待了三分钟。

00:32:03.320 --> 00:32:04.190
<v Ann>Are you married?
<v Ann>你们结婚了吗？

00:32:04.190 --> 00:32:17.990
<v Dave Hill>Well, we're like, no. We're not married. How would you say, as long as we're on the topic, how do you think we're doing as a couple? Like just from vibes, just work off of vibes. Go with your gut.
<v Dave Hill>嗯，我们，没有，我们没结婚。既然聊到这个话题，你觉得我们这对怎么样？就从感觉上，直觉走。跟着直觉走。

00:32:17.990 --> 00:32:18.390
<v Ann>You are a lovely couple.
<v Ann>你们是一对很可爱的情侣。

00:32:18.390 --> 00:32:19.590
<v Ken Perkins>I think you're charming
<v Ken Perkins>我觉得你很有魅力。

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:21.680
<v Dave Hill>Really? Just me charming, or together charming?
<v Dave Hill>真的吗？只有我很有魅力，还是我们俩在一起有魅力？

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:23.830
<v Ann>You should have beautiful children together.
<v Ann>你们应该会有漂亮的孩子。

00:32:23.830 --> 00:32:28.070
<v Dave Hill>That's why we brought the tent. No, I'm just kidding. I'm going to play it slow.
<v Dave Hill>这就是我们带帐篷的原因。不，我开玩笑的。我会慢慢来。

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:31.990
<v Shaina Feinberg>Oh, we're not really a couple.
<v Shaina Feinberg>哦，我们其实不是一对。

00:32:31.990 --> 00:32:33.660
<v Dave Hill>Taco Bell?
<v Dave Hill>塔可钟？

00:32:33.660 --> 00:32:35.040
<v Ken Perkins>That's my favorite.
<v Ken Perkins>那是我的最爱。

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:41.040
<v Dave Hill>Taco Bell's your favorite? I'm going to pretend I didn't hear that.
<v Dave Hill>塔可钟是你的最爱？我假装没听见。

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:42.200
<v Dave Hill>What is your name?
<v Dave Hill>你叫什么名字？

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:42.630
<v Veggie Oil Man>John.
<v Veggie Oil Man>约翰。

00:32:42.630 --> 00:32:45.560
<v Dave Hill>No, not that name. The cool one.
<v Dave Hill>不，不是那个名字。那个酷的。

00:32:45.560 --> 00:32:48.600
<v Veggie Oil Man>Veggie Oil Man, Clearwater, Florida.
<v Veggie Oil Man>植物油人，佛罗里达州克利尔沃特。

00:32:48.600 --> 00:32:52.160
<v Dave Hill>That's a pretty long nickname. Veggie OIl Man, Clearwater--
<v Dave Hill>那是相当长的绰号。植物油人，克利尔沃特——

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:52.860
<v Veggie Oil Man>Florida.
<v Veggie Oil Man>佛罗里达。

00:32:52.860 --> 00:32:54.680
<v Dave Hill>What about such Vegman300?
<v Dave Hill>那Vegman300怎么样？

00:32:54.680 --> 00:32:56.020
<v Veggie Oil Man>U.S.A.
<v Veggie Oil Man>美国。

00:32:56.020 --> 00:32:58.150
<v Dave Hill>Do you love Chick-Fil-A?
<v Dave Hill>你爱Chick-Fil-A吗？

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:02.150
<v Veggie Oil Man>I kind of do Been to about 32 grand openings.
<v Veggie Oil Man>我挺爱的。参加过大约32次盛大开业典礼。

00:33:02.150 --> 00:33:12.930
<v Dave Hill>32? Now, wouldn't the thing to do, rather than spend the money on all that travelling, is just save that money and put that money towards the chicken?
<v Dave Hill>32次？那么，比起把钱花在到处旅游上，把钱存下来留着买鸡肉不是更好吗？

00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:17.470
<v Veggie Oil Man>Travel's free. I got a Mercedes that runs on used cooking oil for free.
<v Veggie Oil Man>旅游是免费的。我有一辆奔驰车，是用免费的旧烹饪油跑的。

00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:19.580
<v Dave Hill>You have a what?
<v Dave Hill>你有什么车？

00:33:19.580 --> 00:33:26.930
<v Veggie Oil Man>I got a Mercedes, a diesel Mercedes. An '83 station wagon that runs on used cooking oil for free.
<v Veggie Oil Man>我有一辆奔驰，柴油奔驰。一辆83年的旅行车，用免费的旧烹饪油跑。

00:33:26.930 --> 00:33:28.860
<v Dave Hill>That's what you drove here tonight in?
<v Dave Hill>你今晚就是开这车来的？

00:33:28.860 --> 00:33:32.040
<v Veggie Oil Man>Yeah. That's what I go to the grand openings in.
<v Veggie Oil Man>是啊，我就是开这车去参加盛大开业典礼的。

00:33:32.040 --> 00:33:35.320
<v Shaina Feinberg>Do you think anything crazy's going to happen in the middle of the night?
<v Shaina Feinberg>你觉得深夜会发生什么疯狂的事吗？

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:37.630
<v Veggie Oil Man>I don't know. This is a bad neighborhood.
<v Veggie Oil Man>我不知道，这是个糟糕的社区。

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:38.400
<v Dave Hill>Is it?
<v Dave Hill>是吗？

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:43.360
<v Veggie Oil Man>Yeah. As long as you stay in the parking lot, you'd be OK. But I wouldn't go out walking around outside.
<v Veggie Oil Man>是的。只要你待在停车场里，就没问题。但我不会去外面走。

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:45.080
<v Dave Hill>Is there a lot of riffraff around here?
<v Dave Hill>这附近有很多流氓吗？

00:33:45.080 --> 00:33:45.950
<v Veggie Oil Man>Yeah.
<v Veggie Oil Man>有。

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:47.830
<v Dave Hill>Is there a lot of riffraff here?
<v Dave Hill>这里有很多流氓吗？

00:33:47.830 --> 00:33:50.910
<v Veggie Oil Man>They said the hood is right across the street a couple blocks.
<v Veggie Oil Man>他们说黑人聚居区就在街对面几个街区远。

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:51.890
<v Dave Hill>The hood?
<v Dave Hill>黑人聚居区？

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:52.640
<v Veggie Oil Man>Yeah.
<v Veggie Oil Man>是啊。

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:54.050
<v Dave Hill>What do you mean, the hood?
<v Dave Hill>你说的黑人聚居区是什么意思？

00:33:54.050 --> 00:33:59.770
<v Veggie Oil Man>You know. And across the street over the other way is the redneck trailer park.
<v Veggie Oil Man>你知道的。街对面那边是乡巴佬拖车公园。

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.330
<v Dave Hill>What what goes on over there?
<v Dave Hill>那边发生什么？

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:05.910
<v Veggie Oil Man>I don't know, but we've seen a lot of people over there earlier today.
<v Veggie Oil Man>我不知道，但今天早些时候我们看到很多人在那。

00:34:05.910 --> 00:34:08.110
<v Dave Hill>Oh, you went over to the redneck trailer park?
<v Dave Hill>哦，你去过那个乡巴佬拖车公园？

00:34:08.110 --> 00:34:14.260
<v Veggie Oil Man>No, we seen them when we got our food at the drive-through. They were hanging out over there drinking beer on the porch.
<v Veggie Oil Man>没有，我们在得来速买食物时看到的。他们在那上面喝啤酒。

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:18.330
<v Veggie Oil Man>I'm going to sleep.
<v Veggie Oil Man>我要去睡觉了。

00:34:18.330 --> 00:34:21.530
<v Shaina Feinberg>Hello? OK. What's your name?
<v Shaina Feinberg>喂？好吧。你叫什么名字？

00:34:21.530 --> 00:34:25.170
<v Cole Naismith>My name's Cole Naismith.
<v Cole Naismith>我叫科尔·奈史密斯。

00:34:25.170 --> 00:34:27.080
<v Shaina Feinberg>And what time is it? Do you know what time it is?
<v Shaina Feinberg>现在几点了？你知道现在几点吗？

00:34:27.080 --> 00:34:31.180
<v Cole Naismith>It's probably about 1:15.
<v Cole Naismith>大概1:15左右。

00:34:31.180 --> 00:34:33.470
<v Shaina Feinberg>Is it like what you expected?
<v Shaina Feinberg>这和你想的一样吗？

00:34:33.470 --> 00:34:51.980
<v Cole Naismith>I didn't know what to expect. I looked around and I thought, there's definitely a type here. And you're probably going to ask follow up questions about what that means. Because I feel like-- I feel like I don't want to be the type.
<v Cole Naismith>我不知道该期待什么。我环顾四周，心想，这里的人确实有某种类型。你可能还会问一些关于那意味着什么的后续问题。因为我觉得——我觉得我不想成为那种类型。

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:56.590
<v Shaina Feinberg>What is the type?
<v Shaina Feinberg>那是什么类型？

00:34:56.590 --> 00:35:33.880
<v Cole Naismith>I feel like most of the people that I've seen here seem to be a little bit twiddling their thumbs in life, not just for 24 hours? Something as ridiculous as sitting out in front of a Chick-Fil-A makes you ask questions about, am I wasting my life? I don't want to look around and seem like I'm judging others' actions. But it definitely has caused me to be introspective about my own.
<v Cole Naismith>我觉得我在这里看到的大多数人似乎在生活中都有点无所事事，不仅仅是这24小时内？像坐在Chick-Fil-A前面做这种荒谬的事，让你质疑自己是否在浪费生命。我不想环顾四周，看起来像是在评判别人的行为。但它确实让我对自己的行为进行了反思。

00:35:33.880 --> 00:36:11.320
<v Dave Hill>Oh hey. This is Dave. It's about 4:15 AM and there's not much action going on right now. Shaina's back in the tent. Not sure what she's doing. Probably thinking about me, I guess. Tension's been-- not a bad tension. Just excitement, I guess, between us. It's been pretty incredible.
<v Dave Hill>哦，嘿。我是戴夫。现在大约凌晨4:15，没什么动静。沙娜在帐篷里。不知道她在做什么，大概在想我吧。紧绷感一直——不是坏的紧张，只是一种兴奋感，我觉得，在我们之间。真是太不可思议了。

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:50.050
<v Shaina Feinberg>It's now 4:15, and everybody is sleeping, for the most part. I think it's going pretty well. I like Dave. He's so nice. Wish I could figure out if he was straight or gay. I can't really figure it out. But if I figured out if he was straight or gay, then I would totally set him up with someone. Just have to figure out what his type is, you know?
<v Shaina Feinberg>现在4:15了，大家基本上都睡着了。我觉得进展很顺利。我喜欢戴夫，他真好。希望我知道他是异性恋还是同性恋。我真的搞不清楚。但如果我能搞清楚，我就一定会给他介绍个对象。只需要弄清楚他喜欢哪种类型，你知道吧？

00:36:50.050 --> 00:36:52.500
<v Dave Hill>OK. Check this out.
<v Dave Hill>好了，看看这个。

00:36:52.500 --> 00:36:58.380
<v Jason>Have you ever looked with love in your heart?
<v Jason>你有怀着爱心看过世界吗？

00:36:58.380 --> 00:37:07.110
<v Dave Hill>This guy, it's like five in the morning. And this guy just came from out of nowhere and is screaming and preaching.
<v Dave Hill>这人，才五点钟。这人不知从哪冒出来，在那尖叫传道。

00:37:07.110 --> 00:37:09.000
<v Jason>Have you ever stolen anything?
<v Jason>你有没有偷过东西？

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:09.460
<v Man 3>Yes!
<v Man 3>是的！

00:37:09.460 --> 00:37:13.010
<v Jason>Well, the Bible says that all thieves will end up in hell.
<v Jason>好吧，圣经说所有的盗贼最终都会下地狱。

00:37:13.010 --> 00:37:14.370
<v Man 3>I'm going to hell.
<v Man 3>我要下地狱了。

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:33.490
<v Jason>Now folks, have you ever used God's name in a curse word? I want you to think about it like that. You don't curse Hitler's name. You don't curse bin Laden's name. But you use the name of God who gave you life and breath, who has allowed Chick-Fil-A to give away free meals, and you curse his name! Jesus Christ out on the cross! He paid--
<v Jason>各位，你们有没有在诅咒中用过神的名字？我希望你们好好想想。你们不会诅咒希特勒的名字。你们不会诅咒本·拉登的名字。但你们却用了赐予你们生命和气息的神的名字，他允许Chick-Fil-A提供免费餐点，而你们却在诅咒他的名！耶稣基督被钉在十字架上！他付出了——

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:34.710
<v Dave Hill>Excuse me--oh.
<v Dave Hill>打扰一下——哦。

00:37:34.710 --> 00:37:48.970
<v Jason>For your sins and my sins! Folks, did you know the Bible says that God sees hatred as murder? A lot of you right now hate me this morning for coming out here and sharing the gospel with you, therefore committed murder in your hearts!
<v Jason>为了你的罪和我的罪！伙计们，你们知道圣经说神将仇恨视为谋杀吗？你们很多人现在因为我今早出来与你们分享福音而恨我，因此你们心里已经犯了谋杀罪！

00:37:48.970 --> 00:37:49.860
<v Dave Hill>Excuse me.
<v Dave Hill>打扰一下。

00:37:49.860 --> 00:37:52.950
<v Jason>All the sins of the world were placed upon Jesus Christ.
<v Jason>世上所有的罪都归在了耶稣基督身上。

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:55.470
<v Dave Hill>Oh, excuse me. Can I interview you?
<v Dave Hill>哦，打扰一下。我可以采访你吗？

00:37:55.470 --> 00:37:57.560
<v Jason>Sure.
<v Jason>当然。

00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:00.030
<v Dave Hill>Um, what brings you to Chick-Fil-A?
<v Dave Hill>嗯，是什么把你带到Chick-Fil-A来的？

00:38:00.030 --> 00:38:01.550
<v Jason>To preach the gospel.
<v Jason>来传播福音。

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:05.290
<v Dave Hill>How did you know to come now? Did you come because, were you just driving by?
<v Dave Hill>你怎么知道现在过来？你是因为只是开车路过吗？

00:38:05.290 --> 00:38:06.830
<v Jason>No, because I knew there was a crowd.
<v Jason>不，因为我知道这有人群。

00:38:06.830 --> 00:38:08.270
<v Dave Hill>Oh, OK.
<v Dave Hill>哦，好吧。

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:09.290
<v Jason>I'm Jason, by the way.
<v Jason>顺便说一下，我是杰森。

00:38:09.290 --> 00:38:11.470
<v Dave Hill>Oh, hey. Oh, yeah, I saw the name tag. I'm Dave.
<v Dave Hill>哦，嘿。哦，是的，我看到名牌了。我是戴夫。

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:13.360
<v Jason>Nice to meet you, Dave.
<v Jason>很高兴见到你，戴夫。

00:38:13.360 --> 00:38:15.390
<v Dave Hill>Is this your first Chick-Fil-A opening?
<v Dave Hill>这是你第一次参加Chick-Fil-A的开业典礼吗？

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:17.580
<v Jason>Yes. I woke them up for sure.
<v Jason>是的。我确实把他们叫醒了。

00:38:17.580 --> 00:38:19.880
<v Dave Hill>Yeah. That was kind of funny.
<v Dave Hill>是的，那挺逗的。

00:38:19.880 --> 00:38:21.630
<v Jason>I'm always a little loud.
<v Jason>我总是声音有点大。

00:38:21.630 --> 00:38:31.000
<v Dave Hill>Do you do this sort of thing often? Like go to, doesn't have to be a fast food restaurant, but like in public, yelling?
<v Dave Hill>你经常做这种事吗？比如去，不一定是快餐店，但在公共场合大喊？

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:34.940
<v Jason>Well, I try not to make it look like I'm yelling. I'm trying to preach.
<v Jason>嗯，我尽量不让自己看起来像在大喊。我在尝试传教。

00:38:34.940 --> 00:38:40.230
<v Dave Hill>Well, not yelling. You're just making it so people can hear your voice in a loud way.
<v Dave Hill>嗯，不是大喊。你只是在让人们以大声的方式听到你的声音。

00:38:40.230 --> 00:38:45.680
<v Jason>I woke up my wife this morning. I said, babe, I'm going to Chick-Fil-A, and I'll be back right before I've got to go to work.
<v Jason>今天早上我把我妻子叫醒了。我说，宝贝，我要去Chick-Fil-A，我得在上班前赶回来。

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:48.360
<v Dave Hill>And she was like, all right. I'm going back to bed.
<v Dave Hill>她说，好吧，我要回去睡觉。

00:38:48.360 --> 00:38:57.880
<v Jason>She's like, what? Yeah. Then she went back to bed.
<v Jason>她是说，什么？是的，然后她又去睡觉了。

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:06.700
<v Shaina Feinberg>OK. It is now 5:40 in the morning, and people are lined up to get their 52 coupons. And everyone is looking really, incredibly jazzed.
<v Shaina Feinberg>好了，现在是清晨5:40，人们排着队领取他们的52张优惠券。每个人看起来都非常、非常兴奋。

00:39:06.700 --> 00:39:24.000
<v Man 4>When I say count, we're going to count down from five, all right? Five! Four! Three! Two! One! Eat more chicken!
<v Man 4>当我喊数字时，我们从五开始倒数，好吗？五！四！三！二！一！多吃鸡肉！

00:39:24.000 --> 00:39:27.490
<v Dave Hill>Hey Shaina, let's sing the song we wrote. Ready?
<v Dave Hill>嘿，沙娜，我们唱写的那首歌吧。准备好了吗？

00:39:27.490 --> 00:39:27.950
<v Shaina Feinberg>Yeah.
<v Shaina Feinberg>好。

00:39:27.950 --> 00:39:31.280
<v Dave Hill>All right. Here we go.
<v Dave Hill>好的。我们开始。

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:31.740
<v Dave Hill>[SINGING]
<v Dave Hill>[歌唱]

00:39:31.740 --> 00:39:34.580
<v Dave Hill>Chick-Fil-A.
<v Dave Hill>Chick-Fil-A。

00:39:34.580 --> 00:39:36.530
<v Shaina Feinberg>Chick-Fil-A.
<v Shaina Feinberg>Chick-Fil-A。

00:39:36.530 --> 00:39:39.050
<v Dave Hill>If you like chicken, go to--
<v Dave Hill>如果你喜欢鸡肉，就去——

00:39:39.050 --> 00:39:40.760
<v Shaina Feinberg>Chick-Fil-A.
<v Shaina Feinberg>Chick-Fil-A。

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:43.040
<v Dave Hill>Chick-Fil-A.
<v Dave Hill>Chick-Fil-A。

00:39:43.040 --> 00:39:44.670
<v Shaina Feinberg>Chick-Fil-A.
<v Shaina Feinberg>Chick-Fil-A。

00:39:44.670 --> 00:39:47.240
<v Dave Hill>If you like chicken, why not--
<v Dave Hill>如果你喜欢鸡肉，为什么不——

00:39:47.240 --> 00:39:49.850
<v Shaina Feinberg>Go to Chick-Fil-A!
<v Shaina Feinberg>去Chick-Fil-A！

00:39:49.850 --> 00:40:04.850
<v Dave Hill>Waffle fries and lemonade. These are just a couple of the items that they have available at Chick-Fil-A. And at first that carrot and raisin salad is a little bit suspicious.
<v Dave Hill>华夫薯条和柠檬水。这些只是Chick-Fil-A提供的部分餐点。起初那份胡萝卜葡萄干沙拉有点可疑。

00:40:04.850 --> 00:40:09.050
<v Dave Hill>But you know what, I've tried it.  And as it turns out--
<v Dave Hill>但你知道吗，我试过了。结果发现——

00:40:09.050 --> 00:40:10.780
<v Shaina Feinberg>Quite delicious.
<v Shaina Feinberg>非常美味。

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:15.420
<v Dave Hill>That's right. Chick-Fil-A, Chick-Fil-A...
<v Dave Hill>没错。Chick-Fil-A，Chick-Fil-A...

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:15.780
<v Dave Hill>[END SINGING]
<v Dave Hill>[歌唱结束]

00:40:15.780 --> 00:40:21.630
<v Ira Glass>Dave Hill and Shaina Feinberg.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫·希尔和沙娜·费恩伯格。

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:24.580
<v Ira Glass>Act Four. Midnight Run.
<v Ira Glass>第四幕：午夜奔袭。

00:40:24.580 --> 00:41:03.970
<v Ira Glass>A little while back, after we did a program that all took place at a rest stop on the side of the highway, we got an email from specialist Lindsay Freeland, who's in the Oregon National Guard, and who heard the episode by podcast from where she's stationed in Iraq. She wrote to us about the road trips that she goes on in her job, in the middle of the night, without real rest stops, to forward operating bases across the country. Quote, "It's late, it's dark, and it's not the normal to be up and driving to a destination. Here in Iraq, this is our military time for driving, late at night, due to the agreement between the American military and the Iraqi military to conduct most convoy operations at night to bring a normalcy back to the Iraqi local nationals."
<v Ira Glass>不久前，在我们制作了一期全部在公路休息站录制的节目后，收到了一封来自国民警卫队专员琳赛·弗里兰的电子邮件，她是通过播客收听这期节目的，当时她驻扎在伊拉克。她写信告诉我们她在深夜里、没有真正休息的情况下，前往全国各前沿作战基地的护送任务。引用：“时间很晚了，天很黑，熬夜开车去目的地并不正常。在伊拉克，这是我们军事行动的时间，由于美军和伊拉克军方达成协议，大多数车队行动在晚上进行，以恢复伊拉克当地民众的正常生活。”

00:41:03.970 --> 00:41:22.490
<v Ira Glass>One of our producers, Nancy Updike, reached Lindsay Freeland on the phone and learned that she's 24. Just got to Iraq in July. Started doing these convoys in August, mostly transporting fuel. There are three people in her vehicle. She's a driver. There's also a gunner and a commander. And it's so noisy that they have to talk to each other over headsets for the whole trip.
<v Ira Glass>我们的制片人南希·厄普代克通过电话联系上了琳赛·弗里兰，了解到她24岁。七月才到伊拉克，八月开始执行这些护送任务，主要运输燃料。她的车里有三个人。她是司机，还有一名炮手和一名指挥官。由于太吵，他们全程必须戴着耳机交流。

00:41:22.490 --> 00:41:32.910
<v Ira Glass>What's amazing is how huge these convoys are-- 20 to 100 trucks, plus another 11 military vehicles providing security, which is what Lindsay's in.
<v Ira Glass>令人惊奇的是这些车队规模有多大——20到100辆卡车，加上另外11辆负责安保的军用车辆，也就是琳赛所在的车队。

00:41:32.910 --> 00:42:01.720
<v Lindsay Freeland>It's a pretty amazing scene. I mean, especially when I'm like the front truck, and I can see this line of lights behind me, because we're so tightly compact within our convoy that we brighten up that whole night road. I always think of it that we're stringing through Iraq like Christmas tree lights. A bunch of white, big, bright lights snaking through the dark desert. And we usually are the biggest light out there, because not many local nationals like to drive at night.
<v Lindsay Freeland>场面非常震撼。尤其是当我在前导车上时，可以看到身后这一长串灯光，因为车队行驶得很紧凑，照亮了整个夜路。我常觉得我们就像圣诞树灯一样串在伊拉克。一串白色、硕大、明亮的灯光蜿蜒穿过黑暗的沙漠。我们通常是外面最亮的光源，因为没几个当地人喜欢在晚上开车。

00:42:01.720 --> 00:42:04.140
<v Nancy Updike>You're saying local national, Iraqis?
<v Nancy Updike>你说当地人是指伊拉克人吗？

00:42:04.140 --> 00:42:29.240
<v Lindsay Freeland>Right. And sometimes they're out there. There are not too many of them. And if we do see them, they're usually a lot nearer their own types of Iraqi rest stops, which are like their kind of markets or whatever. So that's the side of the road. Or a fuel station, which can be a tanker truck with a hose hooked onto it with a little kid ready to fuel up to make money for the day, and markets along the back of it.
<v Lindsay Freeland>对。有时他们也在那。没几个人。如果我们看到他们，他们通常离自己的类似伊拉克休息站的地方更近，那可能就是他们的市场之类的。所以这就在路边。或者加油站，那可能是一辆挂着软管的油罐车，旁边站着个小孩准备加油挣一天的钱，后面还有市场。

00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:31.830
<v Nancy Updike>You'll just see these on the side of the road as you're driving?
<v Nancy Updike>你们开车时路边就能看到这些？

00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:41.700
<v Lindsay Freeland>Yeah, exactly. They're just out in the middle of the desert, sometimes in the middle of nowhere. They're usually to the side of the road. It's their version a Starbucks or whatnot.
<v Lindsay Freeland>是的，完全正确。他们就在沙漠中间，有时在荒郊野外。通常在路边。那是他们版本的星巴克之类的。

00:42:41.700 --> 00:42:50.000
<v Nancy Updike>In the email you sent to us, you said that the night convoys were part of an agreement between the U.S. military and the Iraqis. Explain that.
<v Nancy Updike>你在给我们的邮件里说，夜间车队是美军和伊拉克人之间协议的一部分。解释一下。

00:42:50.000 --> 00:43:07.830
<v Lindsay Freeland>We conduct our operations at night. It's just our agreement so that we don't interrupt their life as much. Just because we are a really long and big convoy. So I mean, that is kind of a big deal, and that is kind of an interrupter, I guess you could say.
<v Lindsay Freeland>我们在晚上执行行动。这只是为了达成协议，这样我们就不会过多打扰他们的生活。因为我们是一个很长、规模很大的车队。所以这是件大事，也是个干扰，我想你可以这么说。

00:43:07.830 --> 00:43:17.030
<v Lindsay Freeland>And they don't like us, and you can tell that. Because some traffic, if we're headed northbound, you know, they'll be going both ways in the southbound lane.
<v Lindsay Freeland>他们不喜欢我们，看得出来。因为一些交通，如果我们北上，他们会在南向车道双向行驶。

00:43:17.030 --> 00:43:27.480
<v Nancy Updike>So basically, they would rather cram traffic going in two directions into one lane rather than try to share the lane that your convoy's in.
<v Nancy Updike>所以基本上，他们宁愿把两个方向的交通挤进一个车道，也不愿和你们的车队共享车道。

00:43:27.480 --> 00:43:39.920
<v Lindsay Freeland>Right, exactly. They don't want to be near us. Because we have big cruiser weapons on our vehicles. We have bright lights. We try to be nice and try to turn off the left side or something, so we dim the lights for them.
<v Lindsay Freeland>没错，完全正确。他们不想靠近我们。因为我们的车辆上有大型武器，还有明亮的灯光。我们尽量友善点，关掉左侧灯光之类，把车灯调暗点。

00:43:39.920 --> 00:43:42.290
<v Nancy Updike>So you're not kind of blinding other drivers.
<v Nancy Updike>所以你们不会晃到其他司机的眼睛。

00:43:42.290 --> 00:43:51.250
<v Lindsay Freeland>Right, exactly. But usually they just don't like being near us, so they'll pull off the road completely and just turn their flashers on and wait for us to pass.
<v Lindsay Freeland>没错，完全正确。但通常他们就是不喜欢靠近我们，所以他们会完全驶离公路，打开危险警示灯，等着我们通过。

00:43:51.250 --> 00:43:55.730
<v Nancy Updike>How many of these convoys have you done?
<v Nancy Updike>你执行过多少次这样的护送任务？

00:43:55.730 --> 00:44:10.010
<v Lindsay Freeland>Probably 20 or 30. I mean, it can get pretty mundane. Now on eight hour missions, they're like a quickie, you know, really fast.
<v Lindsay Freeland>可能有20或30次。其实变得很乏味。八小时的任务，就像快餐一样，真的很快。

00:44:10.010 --> 00:44:12.600
<v Nancy Updike>Because how long are the long ones?
<v Nancy Updike>因为长途的要多久？

00:44:12.600 --> 00:44:26.140
<v Lindsay Freeland>The longest we've had is about 15 hours. It seems very, very long and drawn out, and your legs get pretty tired staying in that one position for a long time when you only have one stop throughout those 15 to 16 hours, you know.
<v Lindsay Freeland>最长的大约有15个小时。看起来非常、非常漫长，当在这15到16小时内只有一个休息站时，长时间保持那个姿势，腿真的很累。

00:44:26.140 --> 00:44:51.080
<v Lindsay Freeland>But for the most part, we are pretty energy drink fulfilled, with a cooler next to our gunner's turret, and we just reach behind him, and grab our energy drinks. And I think I drink probably about three energy drinks in one convoy. Which is not good, you know. Not good for you. But it helps. Especially on the boring convoys, and the long, long drawn out convoys.
<v Lindsay Freeland>但大多数情况下，我们靠能量饮料补充体力，炮塔旁放着冷藏箱，我们就在炮手后面伸手去拿能量饮料。我觉得我一个车队任务大概喝三罐。这不好，你知道的，对身体不好。但确实有帮助，特别是在无聊或漫长的任务中。

00:44:51.080 --> 00:45:07.530
<v Lindsay Freeland>I mean, we always usually have at least one or two breakdowns of our third country national supply trucks. I think one convoy, our 15 hour convoy, what we had probably about seven or eight breakdowns. It was ridiculous.
<v Lindsay Freeland>我们通常至少会有一两辆第三国供应卡车抛锚。我觉得有一次15小时的任务，我们大概有七八次抛锚，太离谱了。

00:45:07.530 --> 00:45:13.100
<v Nancy Updike>Is there ever a time, when you're driving or you're stopped, that you almost forget where you are?
<v Nancy Updike>有没有什么时候，当你开车或停下来时，差点忘了自己在哪？

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:42.930
<v Lindsay Freeland>Oh yeah, for sure. Sometimes I feel like I'm driving in, you know, the desert of the U.S., like Arizona or something like that. You know, it looks like a normal highway within the States. Because A, it's dark, and B, it's just a normal paved road. Yeah, it's not as kept up as well as how the states keep it up, and half the road isn't even painted where the stripes are supposed to be, but for the most part, it reminds you of a normal highway.
<v Lindsay Freeland>哦，有，当然。有时我觉得自己正在美国沙漠里开车，就像亚利桑那州之类的。你知道，看起来就像美国境内的普通公路。因为A，天黑，B，就是一条普通的铺装道路。是的，维护得不如美国国内那么好，有一半的路面甚至连划线的漆都没有，但大多数时候，这让你想起普通公路。

00:45:42.930 --> 00:45:58.900
<v Lindsay Freeland>And so sometimes I feel like I'm on a long roadtrip. Sometimes I feel like I'm driving a big freight truck, like if I was a truck driver back home, you know. It's usually a pretty quick feeling, because you can be brought back pretty fast.
<v Lindsay Freeland>所以有时我觉得自己在进行一场长途旅行。有时我觉得自己在开重型货运卡车，如果我是国内的卡车司机的话。通常这种感觉转瞬即逝，因为很快就会被拉回现实。

00:45:58.900 --> 00:46:23.640
<v Lindsay Freeland>There's tires everywhere. They line the road. I mean, I'm not even kidding when I say that. Most Iraqis, they run out their tires until they're completely destroyed to smithereens, and so they'll just leave those pieces on the side of the road. There's no type of cleanup crew to come clean up the trash on the road. There's no type of officer to come pick up the destroyed tire that's in the right lane.
<v Lindsay Freeland>到处都是轮胎。它们铺满了道路。我说真的，一点没夸张。大多数伊拉克人把轮胎用到彻底损毁为止，所以他们就把碎片留在路边。没有清理队来清理路上的垃圾。没有警察来捡右车道上的废轮胎。

00:46:23.640 --> 00:46:38.450
<v Lindsay Freeland>So that's usually just what seems to bring me back to being in Iraq. And then, you know, sometimes I look down-- wait, I have my body armor on. I'm in still in Iraq.
<v Lindsay Freeland>所以这通常就是让我回想起在伊拉克的原因。然后，你知道，有时我低头——等等，我穿着防弹衣，我还在伊拉克。

00:46:38.450 --> 00:46:53.740
<v Ira Glass>Specialist Lindsay Freeland of Alpha Company 141 with the Organ National Guard in Iraq, talking with Nancy Updike.
<v Ira Glass>以上是伊拉克国民警卫队141旅Alpha连的琳赛·弗里兰专员，与南希·厄普代克的谈话。

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:56.540
<v Ira Glass>Act Five. Bump in the Night.
<v Ira Glass>第五幕：夜半惊魂。

00:46:56.540 --> 00:47:17.750
<v Ira Glass>We end our show today with this story about a very common experience-- staying up all night with a sick child. One of our producers, Jane Feltes, tells what happened to her and her parents back when she was six years old and her parents were 26. They were visiting family friends with holidays in the UP, Michigan's Upper Peninsula, which is the part of the state that isn't, you know, the mitten. Here's Jane.
<v Ira Glass>今天节目的最后，我们要讲述一个非常普遍的经历——通宵照顾生病的幼儿。我们的制片人之一简·费尔特斯讲述了她六岁那年，她父母26岁时发生的事。当时他们正在密歇根州上半岛探亲，也就是该州不是“手套”形状的那部分。以下是简的故事。

00:47:17.750 --> 00:47:36.380
<v Jane Feltes>This night, a lot of adults wanted to go out, so my mom volunteered to babysit about seven of us kids altogether. And all the kids' bunks were upstairs in this old, old log cabin, and the staircase to get upstairs had no railing. It was just kind of, shoots out into the middle of the room.
<v Jane Feltes>那天晚上，许多大人想出去，我妈妈主动要求照顾我们总共七个孩子。所有孩子的铺位都在这间古老的木屋楼上，通往楼上的楼梯没有扶手。它只是直接对着房间中央。

00:47:36.380 --> 00:47:41.740
<v Jane Feltes>And I remember we all got our pajamas on, and we all ran upstairs.
<v Jane Feltes>我记得我们都穿上睡衣，跑上楼。

00:47:41.740 --> 00:47:50.000
<v Jane's Mom>And I'm not exactly sure if you slipped or got bumped by the herd of children running up the stairs--
<v Jane's Mom>我不太确定你是滑倒了还是被一群跑上楼的孩子撞了——

00:47:50.000 --> 00:47:50.970
<v Jane Feltes>This is my mom.
<v Jane Feltes>这是我妈妈。

00:47:50.970 --> 00:48:08.080
<v Jane's Mom>But you were falling. And I managed to grab your ankle as your head smashed into the concrete floor. From about eight feet up.
<v Jane's Mom>但你摔倒了。我抓住了你的脚踝，当时你的头狠狠地撞在了混凝土地板上，从大约八英尺高的地方摔下来。

00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:20.660
<v Jane's Mom>And everybody, all the kids were just frozen on the stairs. And-- ugh, this is creepy.
<v Jane's Mom>所有的孩子都冻在了楼梯上。呃，太可怕了。

00:48:20.660 --> 00:48:38.960
<v Jane's Mom>So I had to do something. So I'm trying to comfort you, and then you'd be crying and crying-- I mean, like, screaming. And then you'd say, oh, I'm so sleepy. You'd get really calm and still, and kind of go limp a little bit. And then you'd start screaming again.
<v Jane's Mom>所以我必须做点什么。我试图安抚你，但你在不停地哭——我的意思是，在尖叫。然后你说，哦，我好困。你变得非常平静，有点瘫软。然后你又开始尖叫。

00:48:38.960 --> 00:48:45.330
<v Jane's Dad>My part, embarrassingly, was I was drinking at Pine Stump Junction.
<v Jane's Dad>尴尬地说，我当时正在Pine Stump Junction喝酒。

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:46.850
<v Jane Feltes>You remember the name of it?
<v Jane Feltes>你还记得那个名字？

00:48:46.850 --> 00:48:49.060
<v Jane's Dad>Pine Stump Junction. Everybody knows that place.
<v Jane's Dad>Pine Stump Junction。大家都知道那个地方。

00:48:49.060 --> 00:49:13.800
<v Jane Feltes>So that's my dad. My mom somehow piled seven kids into a car that wasn't even hers, that was sitting in the driveway, and drove us all to the bar. And she didn't know how to get there, but an eight year old in the car did, so he instructed her. And my mom and my dad, and me and my brother-- we just drove to the nearest hospital, which was in Newberry, which was a really, really tiny town in the Upper Peninsula.
<v Jane Feltes>这就是我爸爸。我妈妈不知怎么把七个孩子塞进了一辆根本不是他们的车里，这车当时就停在车道上，把我们都载到了那个酒吧。她不知道怎么去那，但车上一个八岁的小孩知道，所以他指挥她。我妈妈和爸爸，还有我和哥哥——我们直接开车去了最近的医院，在纽伯里，那是上半岛一个非常、非常小的镇子。

00:49:13.800 --> 00:49:36.620
<v Jane's Dad>And you weren't quite unconscious, but you weren't conscious. And you weren't responsive or alert. And when we got down to Newberry, it was after hours in the clinic, and night was following, and the only guy there, physician, was some retired part-time guy. Older fellow.
<v Jane's Dad>你并没有完全失去知觉，但也并非清醒。你没有反应也不警觉。当我们到达纽伯里时，诊所已经下班了，夜色正浓，那里唯一的医生是个退休的兼职医生，是个年长的人。

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:48.440
<v Jane's Dad>So I had just started practicing dentistry, and the things that were just fresh in my mind from the medical school aspect of my education-- so it was very, very scary.
<v Jane's Dad>所以我刚开始牙医实习，医学院教育给我留下的知识还历历在目——所以当时真的、真的很可怕。

00:49:48.440 --> 00:49:48.620
<v Jane's Dad>wait,
<v Jane's Dad>等等，

00:49:48.620 --> 00:49:50.770
<v Jane Feltes>Wait, what kind of stuff?
<v Jane Feltes>等等，什么方面？

00:49:50.770 --> 00:50:03.810
<v Jane's Dad>Well, like when you're looking at a patient, and they're kind of alert, and they're kind of talking, and then one of their eyes dilates, and they start to slow down and slur a little bit, and then the other eye dilates, and they're gone.
<v Jane's Dad>嗯，比如当你观察病人时，他们看似警觉，也能交流，然后其中一只眼睛开始放大，他们开始变慢，说话开始含糊，接着另一只眼睛也放大了，然后他们就没意识了。

00:50:03.810 --> 00:50:05.210
<v Jane Feltes>And was it happening?
<v Jane Feltes>当时发生这种情况了吗？

00:50:05.210 --> 00:50:05.660
<v Jane's Dad>No.
<v Jane's Dad>没有。

00:50:05.660 --> 00:50:07.360
<v Jane Feltes>Oh.
<v Jane Feltes>哦。

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:26.770
<v Jane's Dad>No, no. But I was checking every five minutes. And I would snap my fingers and see if you were-- just because you were just listless. You just were a little while lump.
<v Jane's Dad>没有。但我每五分钟就检查一次。我会打响指看你有没有反应——只是因为你太无精打采了。你就是一团小肉球。

00:50:26.770 --> 00:50:36.430
<v Jane Feltes>So they told my dad that they weren't even going to call a radiologist until the morning to do X-rays. And they wanted to just keep me there, overnight, like, sitting there.
<v Jane Feltes>所以他们告诉我爸爸，他们甚至不打算叫放射科医生直到早上做X光检查。他们只想把我留在那里过夜，就那样坐着。

00:50:36.430 --> 00:50:57.340
<v Jane's Dad>And I just-- I couldn't believe that I was hearing this. That he was going to make us wait until eight or nine o'clock the next morning before we even called the radiologist. This was up in the middle of BFE, and it was at night. And I thought, this is bad. Because she could be gone by morning. And I can't do nothing overnight.
<v Jane's Dad>我简直不敢相信自己听到了什么。他竟然要让我们等到第二天早上八九点，连放射科医生都不叫。这可是位于荒郊野外，而且还是晚上。我想，这太糟糕了。因为到早上她可能就没救了。我整晚什么都做不了。

00:50:57.340 --> 00:51:03.470
<v Jane's Dad>So I called the nurse in, and your mother and I talked about it a little bit, and then we called him in and said, we're taking her.
<v Jane's Dad>所以我把护士叫进来，你妈妈和我商量了一下，然后我们叫医生进来，说我们要带她走。

00:51:03.470 --> 00:51:21.340
<v Jane's Mom>And I remember thinking, we're going to be out in the middle of the UP now. Nowhere near a hospital. In a car with a sick little girl and another little boy. And we have something like a five and a half hour drive to get home.
<v Jane's Mom>我还记得我心想，我们现在要在上半岛中间了，离医院十万八千里。车里有个病重的小女孩和另一个小男孩，我们还得开五个半小时的车才能回家。

00:51:21.340 --> 00:51:31.570
<v Jane Feltes>Home, at the time, was Ann Arbor, Michigan, where my dad went to school and where they knew there was a trauma center where if, you know, worst case scenario was happening, something could be done about it.
<v Jane Feltes>当时我们的家在密歇根州安阿伯，我爸爸在那里上学，他们知道那里有个创伤中心，如果——你知道，如果发生最坏的情况，那里能采取措施。

00:51:31.570 --> 00:51:59.400
<v Jane Feltes>And I remember very little from that night, but just coming to in little flashes in the first hospital, and in the car. And I remember everything looked like I was in that movie Tron. You know, it was all very, like, black, and the edges of everything kind of glowed bright neon colors. And I wasn't walking, and I wasn't really moving much, except to throw up.
<v Jane Feltes>我还记得那晚很少的内容，只是在第一家医院和车里偶尔闪过的记忆。我记得一切看起来就像我在电影《创：战纪》里一样。你知道，一切都是黑漆漆的，边缘闪着明亮的霓虹灯光。我没法走路，除了呕吐外，基本上动弹不得。

00:51:59.400 --> 00:52:25.950
<v Jane's Mom>The first leg of the trip was really-- actually scary to me. Because the UP in the middle of the night-- there's no lights. There's nobody there. It's desolate. And you kind of look for lights in houses. Kind of trying to get an idea of the time. You know, our car at the time didn't have a clock in it.
<v Jane's Mom>旅程的第一段真的——对我来说其实很可怕。因为午夜的上半岛——没有灯，没有人，一片荒凉。你会寻找房子里的灯光，试图了解时间。你知道，我们当时的车上没有时钟。

00:52:25.950 --> 00:52:27.210
<v Jane Feltes>Oh really?
<v Jane Feltes>哦真的吗？

00:52:27.210 --> 00:52:44.770
<v Jane's Mom>Oh yeah. Cars didn't use to have clocks on the dashboard, and I didn't use to wear a watch. So I didn't really even know what time it was. So you'd kind of watch the windows and see if there's people up. Of course there was no traffic. I don't think we stopped.
<v Jane's Mom>哦，是啊。以前的车仪表盘上没时钟，我也不戴表。所以我甚至不知道几点了。所以你只能盯着窗外看有没有人醒着。当然，路上没有车。我想我们没停下来。

00:52:44.770 --> 00:53:00.740
<v Jane's Dad>If we did, we stopped for fuel. That's kind of a long trip. So I know we had to have stopped for fuel. But we didn't really need-- anything. Didn't need anything. There was no need to stop.
<v Jane's Dad>如果停了，那也是为了加油。那是很长的旅程。所以我知道我们肯定停下来加油。但我们真的不需要——任何东西。不需要任何东西，没有必要停下来。

00:53:00.740 --> 00:53:27.030
<v Jane's Mom>It was still really scary. I remember wondering if you were going to be trapped, somehow. Like that same little girl would be trapped with a brain injury. I mean, you couldn't walk at that point because you were so dizzy. But of course, in my mind, I'm connecting that with you can't walk. You know? Things aren't going to work anymore.
<v Jane's Mom>依然非常可怕。我记得我在想你是不是会被永远困住。就像那个小女孩会被大脑损伤困住一样。我的意思是，你当时无法行走，因为太头晕了。但在我脑海里，我把这和无法行走联系在一起。你知道吧？事情不会再正常运转了。

00:53:27.030 --> 00:53:32.460
<v Jane's Mom>And I don't think your dad and I talked about any of that on the way home.
<v Jane's Mom>我想你爸爸和我回家的路上没聊这些。

00:53:32.460 --> 00:53:51.670
<v Jane's Dad>I worried that there was an undiagnosed spinal injury, and that you would have to fight with a wheelchair your whole life. I worried that you'd have migraines and puke all your life. Which you do.
<v Jane's Dad>我担心是不是有未诊断的脊柱损伤，你这辈子都得坐轮椅。我担心你会偏头痛，呕吐一辈子。而你确实如此。

00:53:51.670 --> 00:54:13.020
<v Jane Feltes>That's true. That was one of the main lasting effects of the accident. We got to the hospital, and they did a CT scan, and found that I had what's called a contrecoup lesion in my brain. And I wasn't really able to walk for a couple of weeks. And then I didn't get back to school regularly-- I think I was out for like a month.
<v Jane Feltes>那是真的。那是那次事故遗留的主要影响之一。我们到了医院，他们做了CT扫描，发现我的大脑里有一种叫“对冲性损伤”的东西。我有几周几乎无法走路。然后我没能正常回到学校——我想我请假了一个月。

00:54:13.020 --> 00:54:50.740
<v Jane Feltes>So that first night driving home after the accident just led to hundreds and hundreds of nights like that, where I was up all night sick. I started getting really bad migraines. I would always get them, like, two, three, four in the morning. I would wake up and I'd see these sparkly lights kind of around the edge of my field of vision. Then there's a little bit of, like, TV v static that gets bigger and bigger over the course of an hour until I'm blind. And then it's like worst-- pain so bad that it makes you throw up.
<v Jane Feltes>所以那次事故后回家的第一晚，导致了后来几百个那样的夜晚，我通宵生病。我开始变得非常严重的偏头痛。我总是会在凌晨两点、三点、四点发作。我会醒来，看到视野边缘闪烁着光点，然后就像电视雪花点一样，在一小时内越来越大，直到我失明。接着是糟糕的——疼痛剧烈到让你呕吐。

00:54:50.740 --> 00:55:05.750
<v Jane's Mom>And that whole thing is like a big cluster [BLEEP]. Because you'd come in the bedroom and bend over me and say, I'm seeing those lights, Mom, I'm seeing those lights.
<v Jane's Mom>那整个过程简直是一团糟。因为你会走进卧室，俯身对着我说，妈妈，我看到那些光了，我看到那些光了。

00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:24.370
<v Jane's Mom>I do remember, as a mom, in the middle of the night when you would wake me up about that, feeling like a good mom. Because I just got up. You know? And I just remember as a kid when I was sick, a lot of times people didn't have time for that.
<v Jane's Mom>作为一个妈妈，在深夜你要因为那个把我叫醒时，我确实记得感觉自己是个好妈妈。因为我只是起床了，你懂吗？我记得小时候当我生病时，很多时候大人们都没时间管我。

00:55:24.370 --> 00:55:25.830
<v Jane Feltes>What do you mean?
<v Jane Feltes>什么意思？

00:55:25.830 --> 00:55:48.300
<v Jane's Mom>Here's a blanket. You know? Go get a puke bowl. And kind of being sick by myself. So I know I do remember feeling good that I wasn't going to be that kind of mom.
<v Jane's Mom>给你个毯子，你知道吗？去拿个呕吐盆。只能自己一个人病着。所以我确实记得因为自己不会成为那样的妈妈而感觉很好。

00:55:48.300 --> 00:55:58.410
<v Jane Feltes>It was nice of them to get up with me like that. But after a while, it just started to seem pointless, because there wasn't really anything that they could do to make me feel better.
<v Jane Feltes>他们能那样陪我起床挺好的。但过了一阵子，这开始显得毫无意义，因为他们真的没什么能做的来让我感觉好点。

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:25.410
<v Jane Feltes>My mom says when you do get up to take care of kids in the middle of the night, it can feel like you're the only people awake in the whole world. And that was true the first night, when we drove through the Upper Peninsula. And it was true for all the nights my mom got up with me. And it's true now that I have to deal with it all by myself.
<v Jane Feltes>我妈妈说当你深夜起床照顾孩子时，感觉就像你是全世界唯一醒着的人。那第一晚我们开车穿过上半岛时是真的。我妈妈起床陪我的所有夜晚也是真的。现在我必须独自面对这一切，也是真的。

00:56:25.410 --> 00:56:45.000
<v Ira Glass>Jane Feltes.
<v Ira Glass>简·费尔特斯。

00:56:45.000 --> 00:56:59.030
